WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:01:21.909
[ Music ]

00:01:21.943 --> 00:01:24.417
Satya Nadella: All right.
Good morning!

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:25.486
[ Cheering ]

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.821
[ Applause ]

00:01:26.855 --> 00:01:27.821
Good morning!

00:01:27.855 --> 00:01:29.209
[ Applause ]

00:01:29.243 --> 00:01:32.700
It's fantastic to be back

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.340
in San Francisco at Build.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.070
It's always fun to be at
developer conferences

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.350
at times of great change.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.910
You know, developer
conferences are always

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:46.470
about understanding tech shifts,
understanding the new stack.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.920
But it's also about
really coming to grips

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.850
with the new opportunity,
right, for us as developers,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.980
for the companies
that we work at,

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.470
as well as the broader world.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.600
So today we're going
to unpack this.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.280
This conference is
all about that.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.690
And if there's one key
takeaway, it would be this:

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.560
How do you all participate fully

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.090
in thisFrontier
intelligence ecosystem?

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:14.240
Right?

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.120
It's not about any one piece
of technology that you'll hear

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.370
about or even the
platform itself.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.900
It's about the value that you
can build, you can compound,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.390
you can create on
top of the platform.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.030
That's what a developer
conference needs to be about,

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.920
and that's what
we will focus on.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.610
So let's kind of get into it.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.640
Let's take a look
at this AI stack

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.710
that this conference
is going to unpack

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.750
in a great amount of detail.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.750
It starts, of course, with
the compute fabric, right,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.210
that ubiquitous compute fabric

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:51.120
that spans the edge
and the cloud.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.450
You then have this layer,
which is the emerging layer,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.600
where you have the
models, you have context,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.200
you have the tools the
models can access.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.690
And then on top of that,
you have the runtime

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.040
where you deploy your agents
and applications that you built

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.880
on top of the context layer,
on top of the model layer.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.610
And then, of course, you
have the best tooling

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.570
to do all of this.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.960
And then you have the
security, the compliance,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.010
and the governance, right?

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.400
That's the simplest
form of the tech stack.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.430
But let's kind of start
where it always starts,

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.350
which is infrastructure.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:32.610
And, in fact, let's start
at the edge with Windows.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:38.520
Because when you step back,
the amount of compute there is

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.920
at the edge is actually
astounding, right?

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:47.820
I mean, think about every
NPU, GPU, CPU even, every PC.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.060
If you sort of aggregate that,
that's a lot of compute power.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.890
So we asked ourselves one
simple question, right?

00:03:55.890 --> 00:04:01.470
If we can deliver unmetered
intelligence to every desk

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:02.760
and every home, right?

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.400
It takes us all the way
back to the very beginning.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.480
But that's what we said.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.080
Can we do that in
this era of AI?

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.450
And, in fact, we're,
in some sense,

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.130
delivering that
already today, right?

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.100
When you look at something
like, say, Outlook Summarize,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.580
it's actually using
onboard AI locally.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:25.980
Same thing with PowerPoint alt
text or Team Super Resolution.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.690
It's just not Microsoft stuff.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.760
For example, Adobe After Effects

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.980
or Premiere are both using
Windows ML across NPUs

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.590
and GPUs for local processing.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:40.290
And so, today, one of the
things that I'm really excited

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.770
about in order to tap into
all this compute power is

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:48.750
to expand the scope of Windows
ML and Windows AI, right?

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.270
So you now have the
full install base

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.940
of GPUs that you can get to.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.010
So I'm really thrilled
that every developer

00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:00.210
out there can count on
building for local onboard AI

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:04.590
and then have it run across
all of the install base.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:10.470
Now, we are also announcing
two very cool new models

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.490
that are all going to
run on Windows Inbox.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.530
The first is a new SLM.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:22.110
It's sort of a more efficient
model, Aion Instruct,

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.900
and it's a great
reasoning model.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.350
And then we have the
planning model, Aion Plan,

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.910
which is a local agentic loop.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:30.750
I mean, think about it, right?

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.930
You now have a full
local agentic loop.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.120
You can give it tools access

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.580
and build fully onboard agentic
applications without having

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.340
to round trip to the cloud.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.780
And of course, we said, in order
to really push the limits,

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.830
realize this unmetered
intelligence, there's also a lot

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.600
of hardware that's
evolving, too.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.970
And so, we're thrilled
to see the innovation

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.620
across the Windows
ecosystem, right?

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:02.010
It's AMD with their
Ryzen processors.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.160
The Panther Lake stuff from
Intel is just exciting to see.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.190
Qualcomm announced two sets
of things which are great.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.600
One on the high end with
Snapdragon X2 Elite.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.770
And then on the low end,
even for sub-500 PCs

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.130
with Snapdragon C. So it's
great to see all of this.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:25.740
And, of course, that brings
us to NVIDIA and RTX Spark.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.440
This is a next-generation
SoC for PCs.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.160
It brings together
the CPU, the GPU,

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.340
as well as the AI capabilities
into a single SoC.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:36.630
And that's the exciting part.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:40.230
And it includes unified
memory architecture

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.890
and also the integrated
DRTM, right?

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.800
So you now have all of
the systems innovation

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.410
and the SoC coming together.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.890
And we're really thrilled
that one of the first devices

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:56.490
that we built was the
Surface Ultra, right?

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.340
It's a beautiful device.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.630
And it really brings the
power of NVIDIA together

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.840
with the design and the
craftsmanship of Surface.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.710
It's 128 gigabytes
of unified memory.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.220
It's a beautiful
2,000-NIT display.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.030
And it has an all-day
battery life.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.410
And so, we are very excited
to see this later this fall.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.220
And it's also wonderful
to see all the designs

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:30.210
from all the OEM partners who
are building, taking advantage

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:33.570
of this new SoC and the
new platform and bringing

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.930
out some pretty exciting
machines that are going

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:40.710
to be all available
come this fall.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:44.400
And so, of course, we said,
okay, this is fantastic.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.990
What can we do next?

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:51.570
So, we said, let's try and push
this and push the architecture

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.720
to its limit for
developers, right?

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.970
What if we could just max
the compute, max the memory,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:04.350
build out that developer machine
that's the dream machine?

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.420
And that's what we're
announcing today.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.210
Surface RTX Spark, Dev Box.

00:08:09.244 --> 00:08:10.804
Let's roll the video.

00:08:11.468 --> 00:09:07.504
[ Music ]

00:09:07.538 --> 00:09:08.118
Satya Nadella:
All right.

00:09:08.152 --> 00:09:14.690
[ Applause ]

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.880
Yeah, this is truly
a dream machine.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:24.540
It's got one petaflop of
AI compute, 20 CPU cores.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.140
All of those things
have 128 gigabytes

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.600
of unified memory access.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.440
So super excited about
this coming in the fall

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:35.910
and you can join the waitlist.

00:09:35.944 --> 00:09:37.591
I'm on the waitlist
as well, so --

00:09:37.625 --> 00:09:38.611
[ Laughter ]

00:09:38.645 --> 00:09:40.020
-- we'll get there.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:45.750
You know, so then we
said, why stop there?

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.530
We said, what if we
did one more thing?

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.620
And in fact, I think
Jensen did that already.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:56.340
And he said, Windows is
coming to the DGX station.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.780
So a new, I describe it as
the desktop data center,

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.090
which you can have right there

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:07.740
and running a one-trillion
parameter model locally.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.240
I mean, just to sort of
put it in perspective,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.790
it's pretty close to
what perhaps we had

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:15.000
when we built
GPT-2.5 or 3, right,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.920
one of the first supercomputers.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:20.100
So it's pretty crazy to think
that we've come this far

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:24.270
where you can now have a
data center on your desktop.

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:27.630
And of course, we're extending
the developer endpoints even

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:28.290
to the cloud.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:33.300
Windows 365 now has the
developer distribution that's

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.450
optimized for developer
productivity in the cloud.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.110
So that's the Windows 365
that I use every day.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.590
And it's simply about making
Windows, whether it's

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:46.800
on the laptop, the desktop,
the cloud, wherever --

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.470
the best place to build.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:53.880
And so, to that end, we have tons
and tons of updates, right?

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:57.210
We're starting, by the way,
with one of the favorite things

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:00.480
for all of us, which
is distraction-free

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.280
dev environment.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.100
We're also introducing
an intelligent terminal,

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.350
which has got built-in
GitHub Copilot, right?

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:10.950
Terminal with sort of the
Copilot intelligence.

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:12.630
And of course,
there's lots and lots

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.780
of Linux love
across Windows now.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.320
So you have 70-plus utilities.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:23.190
So GREP, in full glory,
is now available

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:25.680
for regular Windows access.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.960
70-plus utilities, as I said,

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.570
all of that is
coming to Windows.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:35.340
We're also bringing things
from the Mac that you love,

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.370
things like Starship, Z Shell.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:41.250
Homebrew is going to be
native on Windows as well.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:45.150
So you'll be able to
switch to Windows.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:49.380
And today, we're
announcing WSL containers.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.080
This has, I know,
been a point of pain

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.600
when you're managing
all these environments,

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.060
switching all the mental
gymnastics of trying

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:58.440
to keep this straight.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.870
So having first-class support

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:05.550
for containers will really
help us be in the flow

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.730
when you are building
and deploying locally.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:14.820
And to show you all of this
on our new Surface RTX Spark,

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:20.550
I wanted to invite up on stage
Kayla to just take a spin

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:21.750
through all the dev tools.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:22.760
Kayla, go ahead.

00:12:22.794 --> 00:12:29.429
[ Applause ]

00:12:29.463 --> 00:12:31.260
Kayla Cinnamon: Our team
has been working hard

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.750
to make Windows a great
place for development.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.030
And today, I'm going to show
you some major improvements

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:38.670
and delighters you
can try out today.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:41.160
So what we have here is
the default experience

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:44.310
on the Surface RTX
Spark Dev Box.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:45.990
Right away, it feels calm.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:49.680
There's no news feed, no widgets
popping up, no notifications,

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.720
and of course,
we're in dark mode.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:54.570
I'm immediately ready
to start development.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.610
Well, there's one thing
I'd like to change.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.830
I like to put my
taskbar on the left.

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:01.860
So let me jump into my taskbar
settings using the new Run,

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:04.440
which is built leveraging
the architecture

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.500
of PowerToys command pallet.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:10.590
No, no, no, I want it
all the way on the left.

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:12.270
There we go.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.520
After popular demand,
we're excited to announce

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.800
that vertical taskbar
is now available

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.902
in Windows Insider builds.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.760
The new Surface RTX
Spark also has a bunch

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:27.450
of key dev tools already
installed, like Python, Node,

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.430
and many more of your favorites.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.490
All of the developer
goodness, all in one file.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.130
And if you want to get the same
experience today on your device,

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:38.310
we're making this file available
to everyone right now.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:41.190
We have a public repo set up
with the configuration file

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:44.640
and instructions for how to
apply it using Winget Configure,

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.320
which will make the
adjustments to Windows

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:49.140
and install all the tools.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:51.900
Now, one cool thing that I have
running here is PowerToys' new

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.870
utility called "Grab and Move",
which lets you hold "Alt"

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.540
and move the window
around from anywhere.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:59.760
Another tip is that you
can enable "end task",

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:01.470
which lets you end the process

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.560
without having to
open Task Manager.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.960
So let me jump
into my dev drive.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.530
Dev drives run on REFS with
Defender running in async

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.480
and are optimized for
performance when it comes

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:14.550
to development scenarios.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.550
Also, File Explorer
is "Git-aware".

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:19.950
We've got stuff like
"last change author name",

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.070
"last change message", the
status of each file.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:25.020
Plus, my favorite is
that the branch name is

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.970
on the bottom left.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:31.440
So now let's get started
building and open our terminal.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:33.395
This is an experimental
experience called,

00:14:33.428 --> 00:14:34.877
"Intelligent Terminal",

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:38.040
that makes working with
agents even more seamless.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:39.930
When you first install
Intelligent Terminal,

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:42.630
you're greeted with the option
to pick your favorite agent.

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:44.820
I'm going to use GitHub
Copilot for today,

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.970
but you can use whichever
agent speaks to you.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:49.500
Now here in
Intelligent Terminal,

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.480
I have a regular
terminal pane at the top

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.060
and an agent that's listening
on the bottom, and I can work

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.090
between them while the
agent helps along the way.

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:59.550
So for example, here's an
error being generated.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:02.550
My agent pane is able to
detect it and provide a fix,

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.800
which is great when I
don't remember the syntax,

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:07.830
especially for
something like RegEX.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:09.900
So I'm going to
work on OpenClaw,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.050
and I've already built
it using WSL Container.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:16.380
WSL Container is a native
container experience on Windows,

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.720
plus it can leverage the
GPU, which is perfect

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.150
for the Surface RTX Spark.

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:23.520
It can also reference your
existing container files,

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.220
just like the one in
the OpenClaw project.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.830
So here's one of the files
open in Microsoft Edit,

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:30.570
which ships in
Windows by default

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:34.140
and just got syntax highlighting
in its latest version.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:36.210
And then you can also just
see your containers running

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:39.510
with a simple container
images command.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.580
And since we're on
the topic of WSL,

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.980
we're providing a WSL
profile that's designed

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:46.740
to feel comfortable for
those of you who use tools

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:50.190
like Starship,
Zsh, and Homebrew.

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:53.580
So it comes pre-configured with
all of your favorite utilities,

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.640
and it's available in the
repo that I showed earlier.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.840
And it also includes one
of my favorites, BTOP.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:03.210
So the Surface RTX
Spark is designed

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:04.770
for developer heavy workloads,

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.370
including serving large
local models for coding.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:09.750
I've already done
some development

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:12.030
with a 120
billion-parameter model

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.340
that most machines
can't even load.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.500
So here's a quick
view of my usage,

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:18.870
and we can see how many
tokens I've used locally.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.810
So we're looking at about
3.4 million tokens leveraged

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:23.970
on the device itself.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.330
Now, we can kick off multiple
sub-agents using Fleet.

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:29.160
And just so we don't
have to watch me type,

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.230
I'm going to use
Copilot's Voice feature,

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.990
which is also leveraging
its own local model.

00:16:34.024 --> 00:16:36.791
So I'll just hold "Spacebar" and
tell it what I want it to do.

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:41.580
Find any console.writeline
or debug.writeline calls

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:45.000
in the tray and node
projects and convert them

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:49.030
to the standard logger used
elsewhere in the codebase.

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:50.940
There we go.

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:54.060
Now, the main agent will
delegate sub-agent tasks

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:57.930
of appropriate complexity to the
local model, utilizing my GPU

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.240
and making it more
cost efficient.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.120
Now, as developers, while we're
debugging, we're often looking

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:05.970
through log files to
diagnose any issues.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:09.780
Sometimes finding the location
of the log files is a challenge.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.310
I'd love to be able to
just type something

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:14.730
like "grep log"
and find them all.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.170
Ah, sweet.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:20.190
So on top of already adding
curl, tar, and sudo to Windows,

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.292
now we're adding over 75

00:17:22.325 --> 00:17:25.050
command-line
utilities like env, head,

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.690
tail, and touch for
those of us who love

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:30.010
to live in the terminal.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:33.870
So I found all my log
files, but now having

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:36.210
to parse them is the
second challenge.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:39.810
Well, I've actually had Aion
Instruct proactively performing

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:43.530
analyses on my log
files this whole time.

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:45.900
Now I can quickly diagnose
anything that's gone wrong

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.870
in my development, plus I don't
have to worry about token usage

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:50.970
because it's all local.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.790
We can even take a look at
our machine's resources.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.400
The models are loaded,
and you can see 90 gigs

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.860
of RAM being
utilized by the GPU,

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:02.910
truly showcasing the full power
of the Surface RTX Spark.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:06.570
We were able to use three
local models simultaneously,

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.860
unmetered, while going

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:10.830
up our regular dev
flow without a hitch.

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:12.760
That's huge.

00:18:14.417 --> 00:18:20.018
[ Applause ]

00:18:20.052 --> 00:18:23.110
So, I know you're going to love
what the team's been working on.

00:18:23.110 --> 00:18:25.210
We hope it gives you a
glimpse of what's possible

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:27.710
on Windows today and
where we're headed next.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:29.799
Thank you.
Back to you, Satya.

00:18:29.833 --> 00:18:34.709
[ Applause ]

00:18:34.743 --> 00:18:36.510
Satya Nadella: Yeah, that's
really the beginning

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:39.420
of this idea of
unmetered intelligence,

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:44.270
having those models and having
the agents using the models work

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:46.640
in parallel to what
you may be doing along

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:47.780
with the cloud as well.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.990
And that, I think, is what the
platform enables as first class.

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.120
Now let's move to the cloud.

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:57.510
You know, the driving equation
for us remains the same,

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:00.270
which is tokens per
dollar per watt.

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:02.490
How do we optimize
around this, right?

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:04.800
So when we think about
the systems problem,

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.160
we think about electrons
coming on one end and tokens

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.380
on the other end,
and how do we think

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:13.350
about the systems
optimization end-to-end?

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:16.230
It starts with the data
center design itself, right?

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:20.340
The core compute, storage,
network, all the accelerators

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.640
that go into accelerating
each of those components.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:27.360
How do you even think about
the DC-to-DC connectivity

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:32.190
and the networking, as well
as the offload to something

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.480
like the local compute, right?

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:36.540
That's the sort of
systems challenge.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.600
But before we even get
into all of the systems

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.210
and the technology
and the innovation,

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.370
perhaps the most
important design criteria

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:49.110
for us is, how do we earn the
permission from the communities

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:51.770
in which we're building
these data centers?

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:55.070
And that's where these
principles ground us

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:56.870
and focus us.

00:19:56.870 --> 00:20:00.200
How do we ensure that the
DCs do not increase the

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:02.760
electricity prices?

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:06.870
Making sure that we are
replenishing all our water use,

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:09.900
creating jobs in the
local communities

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:13.110
for the local residents,
adding to the tax base,

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:15.900
making sure we are strengthening
the communities by investing

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:19.920
in local training and the
nonprofits in the area.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.370
Only when we live up to these
principles, do the hard work

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:26.490
around it, is when we earn
the permission to go ahead

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:28.470
and innovate and build.

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:32.400
And we've been doing a lot
of data center build-out.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:38.280
Today, Azure spans more than
500 data centers in 80 regions.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:40.040
It's the most
expansive, you know,

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:43.760
we have the most expansive
hyperscaler footprint out there.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.910
And we have added more
data center capacity

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:51.530
in the last 18 months than
the first decade of Azure,

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:53.840
just to put that in perspective.

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:57.050
But more importantly, what
we are building is also

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.310
very different.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.860
In fact, the first 15
years or so, you know,

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:05.090
we built out the commercial
cloud infrastructure for a set

00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:07.110
of heterogeneous, you know,

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:10.530
workloads that spanned
the enterprise.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:12.750
But now when you look at
what we are building, right,

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:14.970
when you think about all the
gigawatts that were going

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:19.920
to come online, really, they
have three dominant workloads.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.770
There is training,
there is inference,

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:24.780
and then there is the
agent runtime, right?

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.970
These are three
dominant workloads.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:30.090
And in fact, when you
look at Fairwater,

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:34.230
it was sort of our
first AI super-factory.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:38.910
You know, it spanned two
regions, Georgia and Wisconsin.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.190
The entire system was designed

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:42.780
from the ground
up for AI, right?

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:45.450
We worked, in fact, very closely
with even NVIDIA on this.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.910
It's a two-story architecture

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:51.210
that lets us essentially
place racks, obviously,

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:54.150
in three dimensions and
pack the maximum number

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:58.080
of GPUs densely with
network access.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:01.290
That means you've really got
fantastic higher-performance

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:05.100
networking, lower latency,
and more effective bandwidth

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:07.290
across the entire cluster.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:10.560
And we're rethinking even
the power delivery, right?

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.680
So we started, okay, how
do we deliver hundreds

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:17.850
of kilowatts per row while
minimizing all the loss, right,

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:19.650
which is the conversion
loss that happens

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.750
from the grid to the silicon.

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:26.190
So we basically sort of even
took a new approach to it.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:28.530
And all of this also changes

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:31.230
with the cooling system,
right, and water.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:34.830
So in fact, the cooling
loop is filled once,

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:37.200
and the data center can
operate effectively

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:38.820
with zero water consumption.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:42.420
In fact, the daily water
usage over the course

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:45.630
of an entire year is
roughly equivalent

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:48.180
to what a single restaurant
would use, right?

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:49.411
I mean, that's --

00:22:49.445 --> 00:22:54.220
[ Applause ]

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:58.290
And when it comes to the
systems and the silicon, again,

00:22:58.290 --> 00:22:59.850
we have a lot of choice.

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:01.500
We have first-party silicon.

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:04.440
We have partner systems.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:07.320
We were the very first
cloud to bring up, in fact,

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:10.710
NVIDIA's Vera Rubin
system for validation.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.660
Very exciting to see that.

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:14.310
We're working closely with AMD.

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:16.170
We worked with them on MI300.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:21.510
Now we're working with them on
their next-generation AMD GPUs.

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:24.120
Maia 200 is continuing to scale.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:27.210
In fact, it's live
in Iowa and Arizona.

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:29.670
We'll deploy it internationally
later this year.

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:33.450
It delivers 30% improved tokens
per dollar compared to sort

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:36.480
of what's the leading GPU today.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:41.010
And we have validated it with
5.5, GPT-5.5, and we are going

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:44.970
to use that to power
Microsoft 365 Copilot.

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:50.520
And so, when it comes to
running these agents,

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:53.400
the interesting thing now
is it's no longer just

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.890
about having an AI
accelerator or a GPU.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:58.920
The CPU is really critical.

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:02.760
In fact, the ratios may be
even coming to one is to one.

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.970
And that's why we are
innovating with Cobalt.

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:10.350
We're announcing the
preview of Cobalt 200 VMs,

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.020
our next-generation
ARM-based CPU designed

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:17.580
for both cloud-native and
agent workloads today.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:21.300
So it's exciting to see
Cobalt make progress as well.

00:24:21.334 --> 00:24:25.410
[ Applause ]

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:28.230
You know, one thing that we've
been trying to make sure is

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:29.880
that it's being optimized

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:31.740
for these new workloads
I talked about, right?

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:32.880
These new agent workloads.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.880
So in fact, Cobalt delivers
50-plus percent better

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:38.490
performance than Cobalt
100 on cloud-native.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:42.870
But we started benchmarking them
using the GitHub Copilot traces,

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:44.070
these agentic traces,

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.590
to see because the call
patterns are so different.

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:51.300
And we're now seeing 33% lower
latency for the agent calls,

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:54.900
14% faster speed, 23%
higher throughput.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:59.340
So this is about the core design
of both the AI accelerator

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:02.490
and the CPU for the agent.

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:05.520
And, of course, when you
talk about AI workloads,

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.890
you need scale, you
need reliability,

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.850
and that's why the network
becomes super critical.

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:15.540
We have innovated with
the MRC architecture

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:18.720
and rebuilt how traffic
across Azure moves

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:21.000
to support AI workloads,

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:24.420
which are fundamentally
synchronous data parallel

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:25.290
workloads, right?

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:30.090
And so, therefore, they span
tens and thousands of GPUs.

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:31.830
And so, you need to
have them coherent.

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.530
That's what the next
Frontier, you know,

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.170
is for us to make
sure we're able

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:39.840
to keep scaling the
network architecture.

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:43.230
And, of course, it's not about
just inside the data center.

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:46.560
It's also about connecting
across the data centers.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:50.640
And every Fairwater data center,
for example, is connected

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:53.820
through our
continent-spanning AI Wan

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:59.610
forming this truly
fungible compute fabric.

00:25:59.610 --> 00:26:03.450
And, you know, all this
innovation is exciting,

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:07.770
but when you think about
innovation, designing for AI,

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:13.980
and I would say, deep
understanding of systems,

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:16.950
you know, there's no better
company than NVIDIA,

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:21.150
and there's no better person
than Jensen to talk about it.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:25.680
So I want to invite, live
from Taipei, Jensen Huang,

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.205
CEO and founder of NVIDIA.

00:26:28.239 --> 00:26:34.386
[ Applause ]

00:26:34.420 --> 00:26:34.930
Jensen Huang: Hi, Satya.

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:38.430
Satya Nadella: Thank you so
much for being at Build again.

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:40.770
I know it's late
for you in Taipei.

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:44.100
I really appreciate
you staying up.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:49.590
You know, I've been looking
at social and, you know,

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.660
and everything people
have been talking

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:54.660
about since your keynote
over the weekend.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:57.840
And suddenly, you
know, this concept

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:03.090
of unmetered intelligence right
at the edge is so hot again.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.890
So maybe you want to
talk a little bit.

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:07.320
You have thought about this,
talked about this, and now,

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:11.310
of course, with RTX Spark
really delivered, I think,

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:15.960
what's a breakthrough system for
AI to be much more ubiquitous.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:19.110
But maybe, Jensen, you can just
share a little bit your vision

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:22.530
around where you see this going.

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:24.780
Jensen Huang: Well, this all
started about three years ago

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:26.760
between a conversation
between you and I.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:31.440
And we were talking about how
we could build a new class

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:35.070
of PCs that's incredible
for designers and creators,

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:38.160
and it would be incredible
for artificial intelligence.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:39.990
And it would be one
of these systems

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:43.050
that has the
processing capability

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:46.110
but also the software
stack that's integrated

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:49.560
into the world's design packages
and creator packages and,

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:51.660
of course, all the things
that we're doing with AI.

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:54.720
And here we are,
three years later,

00:27:54.720 --> 00:28:00.750
we built an incredible new chip,
and this system is supported

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:04.500
by all of this new software
that you created for Windows.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:07.620
And we now have the ability
to have, essentially,

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:11.880
an autonomous agent
running on the PC.

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:13.485
Now, when you take a step
back and you think about

00:28:13.518 --> 00:28:15.344
what does that mean?

00:28:15.377 --> 00:28:17.910
For the 40 years
or some 30 years

00:28:17.944 --> 00:28:19.470
we've been working together,

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:23.310
we went from inventing
DirectX together

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:29.130
to creating now this
incredible computer

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:31.290
that has autonomous
systems running.

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:35.790
The PC evolved from
being an incredible tool

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:41.010
to now being a tool that's used
autonomously by an AI assistant.

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:45.750
And so, the idea that I could be
traveling and I'm on the phone

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:50.730
and I could text my PC and ask
my PC to get some coding done

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:53.550
or some idea that I
have, and it would fire

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:56.520
up the tools on the PC.

00:28:56.520 --> 00:28:59.190
And it would make the
modifications or the changes

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:03.330
or the design that I told it
to do, and it would iterate

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:06.180
with me while I'm
away from the PC.

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:08.430
My PC became an assistant.

00:29:08.430 --> 00:29:09.900
While I'm sitting
there, of course,

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:12.210
this PC would be my
great assistant as well.

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:18.090
And so, this idea that the PC
evolved from a personal computer

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.630
to a personal AI, it's
just really exciting.

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:25.020
And to see it come to life,
Satya, to see it come to life

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:27.060
and actually doing
that, you know,

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.100
so I'm super excited about it.

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:31.500
Spark, as you mentioned earlier,

