00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.730
Now, before I talk about those
awesome new devices you just

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:24.690
saw, let me start
with the "why".

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:27.120
Back at Build 2023, I talked

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:32.040
about the outside AI application
structure, where AI moves

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:34.770
from operating within
the application frame

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:38.370
to operating globally,
working across multiple apps

00:40:38.370 --> 00:40:42.630
and services to connect,
coordinate and maintain context

00:40:42.630 --> 00:40:48.480
across entire workflows,
devices and time scales.

00:40:48.480 --> 00:40:53.280
What if there were an ecosystem
of devices specifically designed

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:57.240
for that new type of application
structure, for those types

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:00.300
of agents, for that
transformational

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:02.360
interaction technology?

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:05.900
That is the impetus
behind Project Solara.

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:09.560
But with so many possible
forms, which one do you pick?

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:13.470
What is the next device?

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:15.900
You see, the big "a-ha"
for us is that it's not

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:18.480
about choosing one
specific form factor,

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:21.240
it is about creating a system
that extends your agent

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:24.960
across a constellation
of devices.

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.450
The next computer
is not one device.

00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:32.040
It is all these devices working
together as one system,

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:37.770
with agents showing up closer to
where and when you need them.

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:42.390
To realize this vision, two
challenges immediately show up.

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:46.580
First, many specialized
form factors already exist,

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:50.690
but often rely on custom one-off
apps and fragmented stacks

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:55.280
that are difficult and expensive
to build, deploy and maintain.

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:58.040
And second, across
every industry,

00:41:58.040 --> 00:42:01.370
people and organizations are
already building their own

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:05.670
agents, deeply specialized and
instrumented for their work.

00:42:05.670 --> 00:42:08.670
But the impact of those
agents is constrained by how

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:11.670
and where they can exist.

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:14.910
Project Solara addresses both
by giving organizations a way

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:18.720
to extend their agents
onto new, purpose-built,

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:22.440
easy-to-manage form factors
designed to reach the nooks

00:42:22.440 --> 00:42:26.280
and crannies where conventional
computers either do not exist

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:30.140
or are not optimal.

00:42:30.140 --> 00:42:31.670
It's a turnkey solution

00:42:31.670 --> 00:42:34.340
for building unique
agent-first devices enabled

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:37.010
by three pillars.

00:42:37.010 --> 00:42:39.470
First, it's enterprise-ready,

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:42.440
enabled by the AOSP-based
Microsoft device

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:44.930
ecosystem platform.

00:42:44.930 --> 00:42:49.460
Second, it has an agent-driven
interaction model

00:42:49.460 --> 00:42:53.100
with just-in-time UI that
adapts to the form factor.

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:55.440
And third, it has extensibility

00:42:55.440 --> 00:42:57.870
so you can bring
your own agents.

00:42:57.870 --> 00:43:00.810
And tying it all together is
Azure, unifying the system

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:03.000
across cloud and device.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:05.900
Okay. That's enough of that.

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:08.120
Now let's talk
about the devices.

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:11.510
Today we're previewing two
very broad categories.

00:43:11.510 --> 00:43:14.880
The first is stationary and
the second is portable.

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:17.520
The first device is
designed for your desk

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:20.250
and it's built on
MediaTek silicon.

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:22.140
With Hello for
Business, just walking

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:26.310
up to the device securely signs
you in, giving you direct access

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:32.060
to your agents, just like
Nathan's about to show you here.

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:34.640
For the information worker,
this means frictionless

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:35.870
yet protected access

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:40.260
to Microsoft 365 Copilot
grounded in Work IQ.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:42.930
And with a simple glance, it
surfaces what matters next

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:46.440
in your workday, helping you
think, plan, and even act

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:49.860
by delegating tasks to your
agents with a simple tap

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:53.190
or just using your voice.

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:54.990
Think of it as a
dedicated, secured,

00:43:54.990 --> 00:43:57.060
ambient device for work.

00:43:57.060 --> 00:44:00.870
It even supports experiences
like handoff between devices,

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:04.890
acting as a companion to
your existing Windows PC.

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:08.190
Or, it can even let you
access your cloud PC

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:10.800
through Windows 365 and
a connected monitor.

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:12.303
How cool is that?

00:44:12.337 --> 00:44:13.486
[ Cheering ]

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:17.908
[ Applause ]

00:44:17.942 --> 00:44:20.580
Now, the second
device is portable.

00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:23.250
It's reimagining a
wearable that millions

00:44:23.250 --> 00:44:27.510
of people use every
day, the access badge.

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:29.850
Built using Qualcomm
silicon for wearable,

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:33.240
this digital badge is a
lightweight form factor designed

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:36.210
for agent interactions
on the go.

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:38.190
And then it's adaptable
across a variety

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:40.650
of verticals and workflows.

00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:44.920
All right, I have here an
early prototype of the badge.

00:44:46.196 --> 00:44:47.786
And so -- thanks.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:51.390
And using my fingerprint, I
tap to unlock the device.

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:55.350
And I have access now to all
my agents in a secured manner.

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:56.490
And would you look at that?

00:44:56.490 --> 00:44:57.960
I already have a task.

00:44:57.960 --> 00:44:59.610
And it says gather content

00:44:59.610 --> 00:45:01.320
for your social media
posts for today.

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:03.750
So, why not just do
it right now, right?

00:45:03.750 --> 00:45:06.120
So, I'm going to hit "Record".

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:09.240
And then now the device's
camera is recording.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:10.380
I'm going to pan across.

00:45:10.380 --> 00:45:11.040
I hope you don't mind.

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:12.450
I'm going to take your shots.

00:45:12.450 --> 00:45:13.650
Yes. Thank you.

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:17.370
Copilot, find some good shots
from this, clean them up,

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:21.480
and then send them to me for
me and my team to review.

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:23.070
All right.

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:24.330
And then there you have it.

00:45:24.330 --> 00:45:27.000
Now my agent's off running
through multiple tasks

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:29.130
to actually clean this up and
send them to me and the team.

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:30.179
That's pretty cool.

00:45:30.213 --> 00:45:35.224
[ Applause ]

00:45:35.258 --> 00:45:39.090
Okay, I know it's a simple demo,
but it's all agent-driven.

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:42.090
And there are so many verticals,
so many opportunities.

00:45:42.090 --> 00:45:44.280
I mean, for example,
imagine in healthcare.

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:46.440
From the moment you
pick up the device,

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:49.560
the right agent shapes the
experience around the role

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:53.730
and the workflow, helping with
check-ins, patient records,

00:45:53.730 --> 00:45:55.350
and critical insights,

00:45:55.350 --> 00:45:59.280
all through enterprise-grade
secure access.

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:00.690
And with the built-in
microphones,

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:04.020
the nurse can start a hands-free
voice-based documentation,

00:46:04.020 --> 00:46:07.050
including diarization
and annotation.

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:09.900
And the side-facing camera
can be used to verify

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:13.350
and document patient vitals
or even scan in medications

00:46:13.350 --> 00:46:15.060
and help verify workflows.

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:17.160
You know, these are
just a few examples

00:46:17.160 --> 00:46:21.780
of how this small purpose-built
wearable can bring intelligence

00:46:21.780 --> 00:46:24.030
directly into the
flow of patient care,

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:27.510
helping nurses access
and gather and even act

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:32.760
on information while staying
present with the patient.

00:46:32.760 --> 00:46:34.110
And while both the stationary

00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:37.440
and portable concept devices
represent a specific expression

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:40.770
of agent-first devices, their
core reference hardware

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:43.920
and software are designed
to be highly flexible.

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:46.770
I mean, just with a few changes,
loading a different agent,

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:49.050
adjusting the shape, the
screen size, the sensors,

00:46:49.050 --> 00:46:52.200
or even input methods,
the same foundation,

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:56.250
the same software can be
adapted for many verticals

00:46:56.250 --> 00:46:59.760
and workflows, such as retail,
industrial, hospitality,

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:01.770
financial service,
legal, and so forth.

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:06.270
I mean, that is the power of
the platform, that flexibility.

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:08.520
So whatever your scenario,
there are thousands

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:10.950
of untapped opportunities
to bring agent workflows

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:15.870
into places where computing
has not naturally fit before.

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:18.840
And while this is an early
look, we're really excited

00:47:18.840 --> 00:47:23.190
that AccuWeather, Best Buy,
CBS Health, Levi's, Target,

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:27.300
and others are working towards
exploring how specialized agents

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:31.650
and devices can improve
their workflows.

00:47:31.650 --> 00:47:35.310
This is the broader
opportunity for the ecosystem.

00:47:35.344 --> 00:47:38.160
Agents moving outside
the app and taking shape

00:47:38.160 --> 00:47:41.520
in devices designed for
a specific scenario,

00:47:41.520 --> 00:47:46.140
a specific customer,
and a specific place.

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:50.610
And for all of you, this
is the moment to imagine

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:54.750
where your agents should live,
what form they should take,

00:47:54.750 --> 00:47:58.720
and what new work
they can unlock.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:03.360
Last week, Satya sat down with
Cristiano Amon from Qualcomm

00:48:03.360 --> 00:48:05.190
to discuss this future.

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:07.560
Let's roll the video.

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:09.171
Satya Nadella:
Cristiano, it's so wonderful

00:48:09.204 --> 00:48:12.120
having you here at
Device Lab and to talk

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:14.970
about these new
reference designs.

00:48:14.970 --> 00:48:16.230
Cristiano Amon: Very
happy to be here.

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:19.560
I have been to this Device
Lab a lot recently.

00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:21.510
Satya Nadella: You know,
one of the things that you

00:48:21.510 --> 00:48:23.885
and I have chatted
for a while is,

00:48:23.918 --> 00:48:25.590
how there's a real
platform shift.

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:28.860
We are moving from building
operating systems,

00:48:28.860 --> 00:48:32.190
devices for apps to agents.

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:34.560
You want to talk a little
bit about how you see this?

00:48:34.560 --> 00:48:36.330
Cristiano Amon: Yeah,
absolutely. In every generation

00:48:36.330 --> 00:48:38.190
of technology transition
we've seen a shift,

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:41.310
and I think this one seems
to be a very big one.

00:48:41.310 --> 00:48:46.590
Agents really changes the whole
nature of the device in itself,

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:48.720
starting with the
fact that, you know,

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:52.650
if the AI understand the world
the way we understand it,

00:48:52.650 --> 00:48:54.600
it's going to be
closer to our senses.

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:57.060
It's going to be closer to our
eyes, to our mouth, to our ears.

00:48:57.060 --> 00:48:59.760
It's going to be
things that we wear,

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:01.920
and it's changing the
nature of the computing.

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:03.660
I think you need a computing

00:49:03.660 --> 00:49:07.380
that is now geared
towards real-time context

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:09.270
and from silicon to cloud.

00:49:09.270 --> 00:49:10.710
Satya Nadella: I mean,
this is sort of one

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:12.660
of the reference designs
we have, which I love.

00:49:12.660 --> 00:49:16.440
There's stuff happening at
the edge here on this device.

00:49:16.440 --> 00:49:17.790
There's things on the cloud.

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:20.820
You want to talk a little
bit about the core systems

00:49:20.820 --> 00:49:24.900
and silicon implications
for such a new ecosystem?

00:49:24.900 --> 00:49:27.150
Cristiano Amon: You need a
very power-efficient CPU.

00:49:27.150 --> 00:49:29.490
The whole silicon
is designed for you

00:49:29.490 --> 00:49:31.620
to have a cloud-native
experience.

00:49:31.620 --> 00:49:32.970
And then you have
a lot of sensors.

00:49:32.970 --> 00:49:33.270
Satya Nadella: Right.

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:35.280
Cristiano Amon: A lot
of sensors for context.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:38.700
It's a much more personalized
and bespoke experience

00:49:38.700 --> 00:49:40.590
than an app in itself.

00:49:40.590 --> 00:49:43.500
And I think that's changing
the nature of devices.

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:45.720
And even the definition

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:48.420
of a wearable platform
is changing in itself.

00:49:48.420 --> 00:49:51.330
And you've started to see those
incredible new form factors.

00:49:51.330 --> 00:49:53.760
Satya Nadella: That computing
platform, one of the things

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:57.690
that you and I have talked a lot
about is, how do you build it

00:49:57.690 --> 00:50:00.240
such that there is an
open ecosystem, right?

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:02.520
Because it's not
about one agent.

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:04.800
It's about any agent.

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:06.510
Cristiano Amon: This
is what makes it very,

00:50:06.510 --> 00:50:08.700
very interesting and exciting.

00:50:08.700 --> 00:50:12.000
Because the smartphone
today is at the center

00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:17.430
of your digital life, the job
of those devices today is

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:20.580
to be extending the
functionality of the smartphone.

00:50:20.580 --> 00:50:23.580
And because of that, you
actually saw that many

00:50:23.580 --> 00:50:25.620
of those platforms
became vertical.

00:50:25.620 --> 00:50:28.710
It was a natural thing to
have a vertical platform

00:50:28.710 --> 00:50:31.980
from the same company because
the phone was at the center.

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:33.611
That changes dramatically --

00:50:33.645 --> 00:50:34.120
Satya Nadella:
Right.

00:50:34.154 --> 00:50:36.498
Cristiano Amon:
-- when you think about agents.

00:50:36.531 --> 00:50:39.660
And agents becomes the center

00:50:39.660 --> 00:50:41.220
of your digital experience.

00:50:41.220 --> 00:50:43.380
And the industry is
going to be looking

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:49.200
for an open horizontal platform
that actually enable the agents

00:50:49.200 --> 00:50:52.830
to be interacting with
the best possible device

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:54.450
for different applications.

00:50:54.450 --> 00:50:55.800
And I think that's
what's exciting.

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:57.330
Satya Nadella: And we
want to make it possible

00:50:57.330 --> 00:51:00.210
for anybody who's thinking
of an agentic system

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:04.560
to not just be bound to current
devices, but to imagine

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:07.290
that there can be many,
many devices that carry

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:10.194
that intelligence in different
contexts. And so, that's

00:51:10.227 --> 00:51:12.540
the open ecosystem
we want to build together.

00:51:12.540 --> 00:51:14.490
And again, it's so
great to be partnered

00:51:14.490 --> 00:51:16.140
with you to get this started.

00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:16.950
Cristiano Amon: That's awesome.

00:51:16.950 --> 00:51:18.510
We're very proud of
this partnership.

00:51:18.510 --> 00:51:20.530
And it's just the beginning.

00:51:21.117 --> 00:51:25.702
[ Applause ]

00:51:25.736 --> 00:51:28.280
Satya Nadella: Thank you very
much, Stevie and Cristiano.

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:30.200
We are very excited
about Project Solara.

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:33.675
It's sort of a new
platform, but perhaps,

00:51:33.708 --> 00:51:37.530
more importantly, it's a
set of new platform rules

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:40.200
that don't, you
know, in some sense,

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:45.420
hem in what you can imagine,
the type of form factors,

00:51:45.420 --> 00:51:47.490
where your agents live.

00:51:47.490 --> 00:51:51.210
I think always whenever these
new platforms have come,

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:53.460
you get to rewrite
even the rules

00:51:53.460 --> 00:51:56.580
of how new platforms operate.

00:51:56.580 --> 00:51:59.130
And that's what we are trying
to get done with Project Solara

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:05.280
so that you as developers and
enterprises have the flexibility

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:08.190
to imagine the form
factors that you want

00:52:08.190 --> 00:52:10.830
and have your agents
be ubiquitous.

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:15.360
Now, let's go up the
stack to the next layer.

00:52:15.360 --> 00:52:18.870
We are building a new
intelligence layer,

00:52:18.870 --> 00:52:23.910
bringing together the models,
context, as well as the tools.

00:52:23.910 --> 00:52:26.970
It, of course, starts
with model choice.

00:52:26.970 --> 00:52:30.180
Every customer, every
developer is going

00:52:30.180 --> 00:52:33.330
to choose the right
model for the right task

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:38.760
and their eval mix, latency
budget, even COGS budgets.

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:43.470
And Foundry, today, has
over 11,000 models.

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:48.360
It's the largest model
catalog out there from OpenAI

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:51.390
to Anthropic and, of
course, even MAI models.

00:52:51.390 --> 00:52:55.860
And just last week, we brought
the OpenAI real-time voice

00:52:55.860 --> 00:52:59.700
models along with Claude
Opus 4.8 to Foundry.

00:52:59.700 --> 00:53:03.720
So we're continuing to bring
all theseFrontier models.

00:53:03.720 --> 00:53:05.430
The consideration, though,

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:09.690
about the models is becoming
now increasingly key.

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:12.930
In fact, from the last
developer conference to now,

00:53:12.930 --> 00:53:15.150
when you're building
any agentic system,

00:53:15.150 --> 00:53:20.610
having this context really
shaped right is becoming

00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:21.300
super important.

00:53:21.300 --> 00:53:24.120
And, in fact, it starts right
at the data tier, right?

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:28.770
The data estate to date has
been built for applications

00:53:28.770 --> 00:53:31.680
that supported these user-facing
applications, right?

00:53:31.680 --> 00:53:35.430
Now you have to change and build
them for agents, which, again,

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:39.600
have very different call
patterns even to the data tier.

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:45.540
Agents are continuously
storing, retrieving, reasoning,

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:47.160
acting, and learning, right?

00:53:47.160 --> 00:53:50.460
That's sort of what's happening
in a continuous loop.

00:53:50.460 --> 00:53:51.780
And you see that today.

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:55.200
Agents are using things like
Cosmos DB for their memory.

00:53:55.200 --> 00:53:56.760
In fact, ChatGPT does.

00:53:56.760 --> 00:54:00.660
Azure Search is
used for retrieval

00:54:00.660 --> 00:54:03.000
of indices and embeddings.

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:05.910
Fabric IQ for the
semantic models

00:54:05.910 --> 00:54:07.200
and the ontologies instead

00:54:07.200 --> 00:54:11.130
of the business logic tier
effectively for agents.

00:54:11.130 --> 00:54:14.070
And also, you have Fabric
real-time intelligence

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:18.990
for all the observability
traces, which now brings me

00:54:18.990 --> 00:54:22.980
to a very exciting new
service, Horizon DB,

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:26.880
which is our fully managed
PostgreSQL service on Azure.

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:28.264
Really thrilled to have this.

00:54:28.298 --> 00:54:31.894
[ Applause ]

00:54:31.928 --> 00:54:35.460
I mean, one of the things we
wanted to make sure is we built,

00:54:35.460 --> 00:54:38.400
ground-up, a Postgres
managed service which was

00:54:38.400 --> 00:54:41.880
for high availability scale-out.

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:45.720
It's zoned-redundant
with automated failover,

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:51.180
128 terabytes of storage per
cluster, 15 read replicas.

00:54:51.180 --> 00:54:54.900
I mean, the read-heavy
workloads you can scale

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:56.880
with this managed service.

00:54:56.880 --> 00:55:00.270
And in our internal testing,
we're seeing something like,

00:55:00.270 --> 00:55:04.770
you know, Horizon is delivering
3x throughput compared

00:55:04.770 --> 00:55:07.230
to any self-managed
Postgres SQL setup.

00:55:07.230 --> 00:55:12.060
So it's super critical when you
think about the scale you need.

00:55:12.060 --> 00:55:15.450
The other data workload
we're also changing pretty

00:55:15.450 --> 00:55:17.880
dramatically is the
data warehouse.

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:22.800
In a world where agents are
constantly querying data,

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:26.220
the data warehouse effectively
becomes pretty mission critical,

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:26.400
right?

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:29.310
I mean, it's one thing to
have it be mission critical

00:55:29.310 --> 00:55:34.800
for users, but when agents need
the analysis done on the fly,

00:55:34.800 --> 00:55:37.200
you know, bringing
GPU acceleration

00:55:37.200 --> 00:55:43.290
to Fabric is super key, and we
are seeing 7x performance gains.

00:55:43.290 --> 00:55:46.976
So it's really thrilling to
see that AI acceleration.

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:50.170
[ Applause ]

00:55:50.170 --> 00:55:52.810
So if you sort of
have the data tier,

00:55:52.810 --> 00:55:59.250
the layer about this is the
IQ layer that we are building

00:55:59.250 --> 00:56:01.495
that brings
together, essentially,

00:56:01.528 --> 00:56:05.790
the model capabilities
along with the data, right?

00:56:05.790 --> 00:56:08.940
So you kind of mix the data
and the model capabilities

00:56:08.940 --> 00:56:12.720
so that you can deliver
that right context

00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:14.670
to unlock intelligence, right?

00:56:14.670 --> 00:56:17.340
That's where -- in fact, if
you want, when people talk

00:56:17.340 --> 00:56:21.060
about token efficiency, this
is perhaps the most important

00:56:21.060 --> 00:56:22.050
consideration, right?

00:56:22.050 --> 00:56:26.730
If you structure the context
right and feed the models,

00:56:26.730 --> 00:56:28.860
then you will, by definition,

00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:31.230
going to be so much
more token-efficient.

00:56:31.230 --> 00:56:34.500
The first domain is the web.

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:37.770
Web grounding is so important.

00:56:37.770 --> 00:56:44.490
You need that fresh,
high-quality and fast web data,

00:56:44.490 --> 00:56:47.760
and that's why we are
really, really excited

00:56:47.760 --> 00:56:50.507
to announce today Web IQ.

00:56:50.541 --> 00:56:53.810
[ Applause ]

00:56:53.844 --> 00:56:56.560
And Web IQ is built

00:56:56.560 --> 00:56:59.680
on our global infrastructure
that's already serving

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:04.710
over a billion users, but
fundamentally re-architected

00:57:04.710 --> 00:57:06.900
for the LLM and the
agentic workflow.

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:10.860
It's model-agnostic,
it's MCP-native,

00:57:10.860 --> 00:57:14.070
plugs right into
any agent runtime.

00:57:14.070 --> 00:57:17.910
It has web, it has
news, images, video,

00:57:17.910 --> 00:57:24.300
so agents can ground responses
in fresh, verifiable content.

00:57:24.300 --> 00:57:29.040
And Web IQ leads across all of
the three key criteria, right?

00:57:29.040 --> 00:57:31.800
It's best-in-class in
quality, it's best in class

00:57:31.800 --> 00:57:34.560
in speed, as well as in cost.

00:57:34.560 --> 00:57:36.840
So we're very, very
thrilled about Web IQ being

00:57:36.840 --> 00:57:40.080
in the developers' hands as
you build out agentic systems.

00:57:40.080 --> 00:57:42.150
Of course, we're
not stopping there.

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:44.790
Beyond the web, every
developer wants to be able

00:57:44.790 --> 00:57:49.200
to ground their agents on
what's the most valuable data

00:57:49.200 --> 00:57:51.180
across the enterprise.

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:55.350
And so, we are bringing
together Foundry, Fabric,

00:57:55.350 --> 00:57:59.970
and Microsoft 365 as this
unified IQ layer, right?

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:04.290
It's a continuously
updated understanding

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:10.110
of your organization to show you
all of this rich IQ in action,

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:12.360
building real-life agents.

00:58:12.360 --> 00:58:14.690
Here is Elijah.
Elijah, take it away.

00:58:14.724 --> 00:58:18.910
[ Applause ]

00:58:18.910 --> 00:58:20.920
Elijah Straight: Agents
are only as good

00:58:20.920 --> 00:58:23.430
as the context we give them.

00:58:23.430 --> 00:58:27.120
Microsoft IQ unifies
enterprise intelligence

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:29.670
for every organization.

00:58:29.670 --> 00:58:33.450
I'm here at a Power Utilities
Control Center and will start

00:58:33.450 --> 00:58:36.412
by running a long-running agent.

00:58:37.650 --> 00:58:40.890
This agent is going to help
us assess the current grid

00:58:40.890 --> 00:58:43.860
operations incident and
produce a brief for us

00:58:43.860 --> 00:58:46.530
so we can respond accordingly.

00:58:46.530 --> 00:58:48.510
Now, I'm going to go
ahead and kick that off.

00:58:48.510 --> 00:58:51.090
And while that runs, let me
show you how we got here

00:58:51.090 --> 00:58:54.180
with context from Microsoft IQ.

00:58:54.180 --> 00:58:57.090
We built our agent in
Microsoft Foundry.

00:58:57.090 --> 00:59:00.510
It's connected to various
tools, and it's also wired

00:59:00.510 --> 00:59:04.770
to a Foundry IQ knowledge
base, a single grounded source

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:07.800
that packages our documents,
operational data,

00:59:07.800 --> 00:59:11.640
and people into context
the agent can reason over.

00:59:11.640 --> 00:59:14.460
After building the agent in
Foundry, we published it

00:59:14.460 --> 00:59:17.790
to Microsoft 365
for the whole team.

00:59:17.790 --> 00:59:19.904
Let's go see it in action.

00:59:21.030 --> 00:59:23.820
Now, here in M365, I will
start with a question

00:59:23.820 --> 00:59:26.790
about current events that an
ungrounded LLM wouldn't be able

00:59:26.790 --> 00:59:27.930
to answer.

00:59:27.930 --> 00:59:32.370
I'm going to ask about current
electricity prices in SF.

00:59:32.370 --> 00:59:37.080
For this, our agent pulls in the
first IQ in our toolbox, Web IQ,

00:59:37.080 --> 00:59:39.510
search built for the AI era.

00:59:39.510 --> 00:59:43.050
Web IQ delivers
industry-leading quality,

00:59:43.050 --> 00:59:45.540
velocity, and efficiency.

00:59:45.540 --> 00:59:49.260
Web IQ constantly indexes
fresh, official sources

00:59:49.260 --> 00:59:51.510
from across the web,
and additionally,

00:59:51.510 --> 00:59:54.870
Web IQ does a great job
with semantic documents.

00:59:54.870 --> 00:59:58.530
And here we can see Web IQ
gave us our answer grounded

00:59:58.530 --> 01:00:00.480
in reality.

01:00:00.480 --> 01:00:03.660
Now, not only can we deploy
our agent here in M365,

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:06.450
but we can also embed
it in our custom apps.

01:00:06.450 --> 01:00:08.970
Let's head back to
the control center.

01:00:08.970 --> 01:00:11.640
Let's see how we handled
a previous incident.

01:00:11.640 --> 01:00:15.210
So, after using Web IQ to
gather external info,

01:00:15.210 --> 01:00:19.890
we asked for details about our
potentially at-risk substations.

01:00:19.890 --> 01:00:23.280
The real power of Microsoft
IQ comes when we combine

01:00:23.280 --> 01:00:27.840
that external knowledge with our
own internal enterprise context.

01:00:27.840 --> 01:00:30.690
For this information, our
agent pulls in the next layer

01:00:30.690 --> 01:00:34.620
of Microsoft IQ, Fabric IQ.

01:00:34.620 --> 01:00:37.050
Here is Brightline's
grid represented

01:00:37.050 --> 01:00:39.120
as a Fabric ontology,

01:00:39.120 --> 01:00:42.150
an operational model
of the live grid.

01:00:42.150 --> 01:00:45.300
And critically, we didn't
build this from scratch.

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:49.200
Fabric takes the Power BI
semantic models used by millions

01:00:49.200 --> 01:00:52.410
of customers today and
lets teams extend them

01:00:52.410 --> 01:00:55.920
into rich ontologies that
help run the business.

01:00:55.920 --> 01:00:57.462
This model -- yes!

01:00:57.496 --> 01:01:00.030
This model is coupled
with live telemetry

01:01:00.030 --> 01:01:02.580
so it reflects the
real operational state

01:01:02.580 --> 01:01:05.280
of the grid minute-by-minute.

01:01:05.280 --> 01:01:09.520
This is what Microsoft IQ means
by enterprise intelligence.

01:01:09.520 --> 01:01:13.760
[ Applause ]

01:01:13.760 --> 01:01:16.400
Not data scattered across
disparate systems,

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:18.860
but a single living
model of the business

01:01:18.860 --> 01:01:21.060
that an agent can reason over.

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:23.580
Now, turning back to our agent,

01:01:23.580 --> 01:01:26.130
we can see that it
gave us a table

01:01:26.130 --> 01:01:28.440
of our most exposed substations.

01:01:28.440 --> 01:01:32.010
That's the agent querying
the ontology you just saw.

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:33.330
Now, the agent has access

01:01:33.330 --> 01:01:35.610
to both the outside
world and our grid.

01:01:35.610 --> 01:01:38.400
The last piece is the one
that turns a situation

01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:42.630
into a response, our
policies and our people.

01:01:42.630 --> 01:01:46.620
By asking, what are the steps to
respond to a substation trip,

01:01:46.620 --> 01:01:51.660
we activate the final layer
of Microsoft IQ, Work IQ.

01:01:51.660 --> 01:01:55.230
This is Brightline's response
procedure in SharePoint.

01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:57.600
It's the playbook the
team actually reaches

01:01:57.600 --> 01:01:59.760
for when something goes wrong.

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:02.460
And the important thing is,
the agent isn't working

01:02:02.460 --> 01:02:05.280
from a stale upload
or copied snapshot.

01:02:05.280 --> 01:02:09.090
It's answering from the same
source the team maintains day

01:02:09.090 --> 01:02:10.620
to day.

01:02:10.620 --> 01:02:13.560
When the procedure changes,
the answer changes with it.

01:02:13.560 --> 01:02:17.370
No re-uploads, no prompt
rebuilds, and no stale versions.

01:02:17.370 --> 01:02:20.970
And critically, this
is your knowledge.

01:02:20.970 --> 01:02:24.810
The assets you create stay
with you, no matter what model

01:02:24.810 --> 01:02:27.300
or agent is reasoning over them.

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:30.810
Now, if we return to our agent,
we can see the proper way

01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:32.610
to respond to this issue.

01:02:32.610 --> 01:02:34.230
Not a generic recommendation,

01:02:34.230 --> 01:02:37.800
but our playbook applied
to this incident.

01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:41.010
Now, we just saw one
situation, three questions,

01:02:41.010 --> 01:02:42.510
and one connected answer.

01:02:42.510 --> 01:02:45.720
Now, let's go check back in
on our long-running agent.

01:02:45.720 --> 01:02:50.760
And, oh, let's check the
backup really quick.

01:02:50.760 --> 01:02:52.450
Moment of truth. . .

01:02:53.665 --> 01:02:55.390
And. . .

01:02:55.429 --> 01:02:56.498
boom!

01:02:57.779 --> 01:02:59.739
Our task finished.

01:02:59.773 --> 01:03:02.812
[ Applause ]

01:03:02.846 --> 01:03:06.930
Here we can see every
step the agent took.

01:03:06.930 --> 01:03:08.700
First, beginning with Web IQ,

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:10.920
connecting it to
the outside world.

01:03:10.920 --> 01:03:14.310
Second, Fabric IQ through
Foundry, anchoring it

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:16.500
in the real state
of our operations.

01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:21.120
And third, Work IQ, grounding it
in our people and procedures.

01:03:21.120 --> 01:03:23.760
I triggered this manually,
but with Foundry routines,

01:03:23.760 --> 01:03:26.760
this can run on a schedule,
turning a one-off response

01:03:26.760 --> 01:03:29.640
into continuous
proactive execution.

01:03:29.640 --> 01:03:34.230
And, if we look closely, we
can see it even used the power

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:37.440
of Work IQ to alert me
directly about the incident.

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:39.420
And if we go ahead
and check Teams,

01:03:39.420 --> 01:03:44.307
it sent me an incident brief
notifying me of the situation.

01:03:44.341 --> 01:03:48.940
[ Applause ]

01:03:48.940 --> 01:03:51.300
That's the power
of Microsoft IQ.

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:53.850
When a crisis hits, the
team doesn't chase answers

01:03:53.850 --> 01:03:55.440
across a dozen systems.

01:03:55.440 --> 01:03:58.680
They ask a question and get a
response grounded in the world,

01:03:58.680 --> 01:04:00.810
their operations,
and their people,

01:04:00.810 --> 01:04:02.520
all in one place they can trust.

01:04:02.520 --> 01:04:04.412
Back to you, Satya.

01:04:04.446 --> 01:04:07.702
[ Applause ]

01:04:07.736 --> 01:04:11.640
Satya Nadella: All right.
Thank you, Elijah.

01:04:11.640 --> 01:04:14.940
And now, you know,
let's sort of move

01:04:14.940 --> 01:04:20.040
up from this context model
layer to deploying these agents

01:04:20.040 --> 01:04:22.200
and thinking about the runtime.

01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:26.550
And, you know, when you're
building a first-class agentic

01:04:26.550 --> 01:04:30.390
system, you need a first-class
agent runtime and a platform

01:04:30.390 --> 01:04:33.060
that we are going to ship
both with Windows as well

01:04:33.060 --> 01:04:35.610
as part of Foundry in Azure.

01:04:35.610 --> 01:04:39.060
We want Windows to
be a fantastic place

01:04:39.060 --> 01:04:42.240
to run and scale agents.

01:04:42.240 --> 01:04:44.155
And agents, effectively,

01:04:44.189 --> 01:04:46.560
are a new execution
environment, right?

01:04:46.560 --> 01:04:47.910
It's a new paradigm even.

01:04:47.910 --> 01:04:50.580
They reason continuously.

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:53.700
They generate and run
code dynamically.

01:04:53.700 --> 01:04:58.304
They take actions across
files and devices as well

01:04:58.338 --> 01:05:01.011
as across the network.

01:05:01.045 --> 01:05:04.080
Obviously, there's a lot
of power in it, right?

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:07.350
The fact that it can
generate code and act on it

01:05:07.350 --> 01:05:09.990
on a long-running agent
that's autonomous.

01:05:09.990 --> 01:05:12.150
But, obviously, it
creates new risk.

01:05:12.150 --> 01:05:16.440
And that's why today we're
introducing Microsoft Execution

01:05:16.440 --> 01:05:18.450
Containers, or MXC.

01:05:18.450 --> 01:05:24.840
MXC is a new policy layer that
lets Windows apply isolation

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:28.740
and containment
using AI-native --

01:05:28.740 --> 01:05:31.350
-- or OS-native
primitives, right?

01:05:31.350 --> 01:05:34.770
You need to bake this
into the operating system

01:05:34.770 --> 01:05:38.550
so that the containment
is enforced by policy.

01:05:38.550 --> 01:05:40.950
You can have
process-level isolation

01:05:40.950 --> 01:05:42.930
for lightweight agent actions.

01:05:42.930 --> 01:05:47.640
You can have session-level
isolation for user separation.

01:05:47.640 --> 01:05:50.040
So Windows and Linux
virtual machines, in fact,

01:05:50.040 --> 01:05:55.380
are also great, including WSL,
for much stronger boundaries.

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:58.200
And, in fact, if you want full
isolation and containment,

01:05:58.200 --> 01:06:01.620
Windows 365 for agents
for maximum isolation

01:06:01.620 --> 01:06:04.500
in a separate managed
environment effectively.

01:06:04.500 --> 01:06:07.290
You can pick the right
containment option

01:06:07.290 --> 01:06:11.580
for the workload, and Windows
will enforce it via MXC.

01:06:11.580 --> 01:06:15.030
And so, I think this becomes
pretty critical as you think

01:06:15.030 --> 01:06:19.740
about deploying agents at
scale on your Windows desktop.

01:06:19.740 --> 01:06:22.620
We want to ensure containment
is enforced, of course,

01:06:22.620 --> 01:06:25.440
regardless of who builds
the agent, right?

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:28.470
So this is why you want to bake
it into the operating system.

01:06:28.470 --> 01:06:31.290
To that end, we are working
with many partners to ensure

01:06:31.290 --> 01:06:33.960
that containment, we
are building support,

01:06:33.960 --> 01:06:36.480
real developer
workloads out there,

01:06:36.480 --> 01:06:38.610
and also addresses the needs.

01:06:38.610 --> 01:06:42.210
In fact, NVIDIA is bringing
OpenShell to Windows

01:06:42.210 --> 01:06:46.260
to securely execute
autonomous AI agents.

01:06:46.260 --> 01:06:50.460
And today, we are really
thrilled to announce

01:06:50.460 --> 01:06:54.506
that OpenClaw runs on
Windows, leveraging MXC.

01:06:54.539 --> 01:06:58.881
[ Applause ]

01:06:58.916 --> 01:07:01.830
Yeah, we are very deeply
engaged with the team

01:07:01.830 --> 01:07:05.370
to make OpenClaw run
super well on Windows.

01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:08.400
And so, let me hand it
over to my colleagues,

