option settings.

01:09:32.040 --> 01:09:36.030
But, Samantha, talk to
me about "Custom folders".

01:09:36.030 --> 01:09:37.770
Samantha Song: Yeah,
you've got full support

01:09:37.770 --> 01:09:41.520
about what files and folders you
want OpenClaw to have access to.

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:44.850
And really granular security
features like "Clipboard" access

01:09:44.850 --> 01:09:47.310
or talking to the
Internet itself.

01:09:47.310 --> 01:09:50.550
Now, I've given it
read-only desktop,

01:09:50.550 --> 01:09:52.860
read access to your
desktop folder.

01:09:52.860 --> 01:09:55.470
OpenClaw already has
a rich safety layer.

01:09:55.470 --> 01:09:59.040
And that layer is only augmented
more by appropriate containment

01:09:59.040 --> 01:10:02.960
that can be managed by me
or policies applied by IT.

01:10:02.960 --> 01:10:05.150
Now, for the purposes
of this demo,

01:10:05.150 --> 01:10:08.000
I'm going to do something
really scary and ask OpenClaw

01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:10.340
to delete all the
files on your desktop.

01:10:10.340 --> 01:10:11.030
Scott Hanselman: That's cool,

01:10:11.030 --> 01:10:13.530
because I have a
nice, clean desktop.

01:10:13.530 --> 01:10:15.300
I think you're keeping a secret.

01:10:15.300 --> 01:10:19.200
You hid all your icons from the
audience while we were on stage.

01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:22.320
I think we need to show the
audience who you really are.

01:10:22.320 --> 01:10:24.270
Samantha Song: So disrespectful.

01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:25.602
I know where everything is.

01:10:25.636 --> 01:10:26.996
[ Laughter ]

01:10:27.030 --> 01:10:28.940
It's just -- don't
touch my stuff.

01:10:28.940 --> 01:10:30.380
I know where they are.

01:10:30.380 --> 01:10:32.480
You know, Sam, I need to
make sure that you're clear

01:10:32.480 --> 01:10:34.970
that a messy desktop
is an organized mind.

01:10:34.970 --> 01:10:38.220
I'm pretty sure that
that's the quote, right?

01:10:38.220 --> 01:10:39.960
So what we've done is
we've asked OpenClaw

01:10:39.960 --> 01:10:42.030
to delete those files
from the Windows node.

01:10:42.030 --> 01:10:45.240
And the only thing that is going
to keep from happening is MXC,

01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:46.290
because we've turned off all

01:10:46.290 --> 01:10:48.690
of the many layers
that OpenClaw offers.

01:10:48.690 --> 01:10:50.670
But our IT -- in this
case, Samantha --

01:10:50.670 --> 01:10:52.410
has set it to read-only.

01:10:52.410 --> 01:10:54.630
So, it's trying to go and
delete all of those files.

01:10:54.630 --> 01:10:56.370
We can actually see the
different attempts,

01:10:56.370 --> 01:10:58.800
where it's going and deleting,
and then checking the directory,

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:01.410
and then deleting again,
because it's very persistent.

01:11:01.410 --> 01:11:05.310
It wants these files gone,
and I want them to stay.

01:11:05.310 --> 01:11:09.690
Oh, nope. The read-only
sandbox is there.

01:11:09.690 --> 01:11:12.240
94 JPEGs are still
on the desktop.

01:11:12.240 --> 01:11:13.020
Absolutely.

01:11:13.020 --> 01:11:17.460
My desktop icons are safe from
Samantha's reign of terror.

01:11:17.460 --> 01:11:18.420
Foiled again.

01:11:18.420 --> 01:11:20.700
Scott Hanselman:
Oh, my goodness -- so bad.

01:11:20.700 --> 01:11:23.040
Samantha Song: Today we've
seen security sandboxing,

01:11:23.040 --> 01:11:26.160
WinUI 3, and open-source
brought together all

01:11:26.160 --> 01:11:29.130
in this alpha release of
a Windows companion app.

01:11:29.130 --> 01:11:32.460
We think this app is a great
opportunity to showcase OpenClaw

01:11:32.460 --> 01:11:33.870
on Windows, and it's only going

01:11:33.870 --> 01:11:35.520
to get better in
the coming months.

01:11:35.520 --> 01:11:37.080
Scott Hanselman: That is
right, and by the way,

01:11:37.080 --> 01:11:39.150
I want to note that we're doing
all this development work

01:11:39.150 --> 01:11:42.870
on this calm Windows development
machine with all the tools

01:11:42.870 --> 01:11:48.240
that I love, like WSL,
Containers, and Containment,

01:11:48.240 --> 01:11:50.070
and I've even got GitHub Copilot

01:11:50.070 --> 01:11:52.200
with multi-model
support ready to go.

01:11:52.200 --> 01:11:55.080
Now, this is literally how the
team and I have been working

01:11:55.080 --> 01:11:57.870
on the OpenClaw app on GitHub.

01:11:57.870 --> 01:12:00.720
Now, people might wonder how
all of this came together.

01:12:00.720 --> 01:12:03.210
Turns out, that
over the holidays,

01:12:03.210 --> 01:12:07.410
I got a DM from this random
guy on the Internet,

01:12:07.410 --> 01:12:10.760
and it took me a couple of
days to get back to him,

01:12:10.760 --> 01:12:14.090
and then when I did, we just
had this kind of cool idea

01:12:14.090 --> 01:12:17.180
that maybe he should
come to Microsoft Build.

01:12:17.180 --> 01:12:19.430
Now, I think there's one more
person that we all want to thank

01:12:19.430 --> 01:12:22.080
for bringing the next generation
of agents to the world.

01:12:22.080 --> 01:12:23.190
Everybody put your
hands together

01:12:23.224 --> 01:12:25.747
for the "Clawfather" himself,
Peter Steinberger.

01:12:25.781 --> 01:12:29.112
[ Applause ]

01:12:29.146 --> 01:12:31.188
Peter Steinberger:
Scott. . .

01:12:31.221 --> 01:12:32.810
Samantha.

01:12:32.810 --> 01:12:35.017
Stop showing off my secret DMs.

01:12:35.051 --> 01:12:36.103
[ Laughter ]

01:12:37.520 --> 01:12:41.660
I'm so excited to see
OpenClaw native on Windows.

01:12:41.660 --> 01:12:46.350
You know, watching a "claw" try
to delete all your desktop file

01:12:46.384 --> 01:12:49.141
and just fail made
me really happy

01:12:49.175 --> 01:12:50.098
[ Laughter ]

01:12:50.132 --> 01:12:52.477
because six months ago,
that totally would have worked.

01:12:52.511 --> 01:12:53.912
[ Laughter ]

01:12:53.946 --> 01:12:58.080
You know, I built OpenClaw to
have access to everything.

01:12:58.080 --> 01:13:01.950
You know, my files, my
machines, my chats, always on

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:03.480
and fully open-source.

01:13:03.480 --> 01:13:05.700
That's what makes
it so powerful,

01:13:05.700 --> 01:13:09.120
and that's what also makes
companies a bit nervous.

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:11.576
You know, what I
kept hearing was,

01:13:11.609 --> 01:13:16.020
"Peter, I love my 'claw',
can I use this at work?"

01:13:16.020 --> 01:13:18.750
And that's what we spent
the last few months on,

01:13:18.750 --> 01:13:24.300
with Microsoft, GitHub, OpenAI,
NVIDIA, just to name a few.

01:13:24.300 --> 01:13:25.770
We added observability.

01:13:25.770 --> 01:13:28.260
We added auto mode
for permissions.

01:13:28.260 --> 01:13:30.090
We changed how access works.

01:13:30.090 --> 01:13:31.890
It's not all or nothing anymore.

01:13:31.890 --> 01:13:34.620
You can pick which folder
should be read-only,

01:13:34.620 --> 01:13:37.590
which one should be
write-it or hidden.

01:13:37.590 --> 01:13:39.270
So here's the news.

01:13:39.270 --> 01:13:42.604
You can totally run OpenClaw
inside your company now.

01:13:42.638 --> 01:13:43.767
[ Cheering ]

01:13:43.801 --> 01:13:47.103
[ Applause ]

01:13:47.137 --> 01:13:51.060
And we even made the
harness itself a plug-in.

01:13:51.060 --> 01:13:52.710
You can bring your own.

01:13:52.710 --> 01:13:57.210
Copilot, Codex, whatever
you already trust,

01:13:57.210 --> 01:14:00.090
and your rules come
right with it.

01:14:00.090 --> 01:14:02.370
And then you put
OpenClaw on top of it.

01:14:02.370 --> 01:14:05.070
You get persistent
memory, heartbeats,

01:14:05.070 --> 01:14:09.930
and you get a "claw" right
inside Slack or Teams.

01:14:09.930 --> 01:14:13.616
It's been really exciting
to see OpenClaw

01:14:13.650 --> 01:14:14.982
grow into something

01:14:15.015 --> 01:14:17.285
much bigger;

01:14:17.318 --> 01:14:20.790
a global movement
and a community.

01:14:20.790 --> 01:14:24.330
And I started the OpenClaw
Foundation, a real nonprofit,

01:14:24.330 --> 01:14:30.900
so it stays open and neutral,
any model, any operating system.

01:14:30.900 --> 01:14:35.730
Because we are entering a new
era of building these agents.

01:14:35.730 --> 01:14:39.120
More capability for the
people who don't code

01:14:39.120 --> 01:14:42.330
and more power for those who do.

01:14:42.330 --> 01:14:43.710
And we get to do this.

01:14:43.710 --> 01:14:47.040
We get to build it
together in the open.

01:14:47.040 --> 01:14:48.870
So my task is simple:

01:14:48.870 --> 01:14:50.130
Come build with us.

01:14:50.130 --> 01:14:50.979
Thank you.

01:14:51.013 --> 01:14:52.892
[ Applause ]

01:14:52.926 --> 01:14:53.695
Scott Hanselman: Fantastic.

01:14:53.729 --> 01:14:55.166
[ Applause ]

01:14:55.200 --> 01:14:56.820
Thank you very much to Peter.

01:14:56.820 --> 01:14:58.080
Thank you to Samantha.

01:14:58.080 --> 01:15:00.750
Thank you to the Companion
App team for their hard work.

01:15:00.750 --> 01:15:02.370
Thank you to the
OpenClaw community

01:15:02.370 --> 01:15:04.710
for giving everyone here a
crustacean of their own.

01:15:04.710 --> 01:15:06.360
And as we walk out,
I want to remind you

01:15:06.360 --> 01:15:08.430
that that's the first time
you've ever seen OpenClaw

01:15:08.430 --> 01:15:11.432
running on a Surface
Laptop Ultra. Goodbye.

01:15:11.466 --> 01:15:17.600
[ Applause ]

01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:19.762
Satya Nadella: All right,
thank you so much,

01:15:19.796 --> 01:15:21.470
Samantha, Scott, and Peter.

01:15:21.470 --> 01:15:26.820
It's so wonderful to see
OpenClaw come to Windows

01:15:26.820 --> 01:15:31.950
and have all of that capability
in terms of the security

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:36.150
and that comfort to be able to
have these long-running agents

01:15:36.150 --> 01:15:39.300
and unmetered intelligence
come together.

01:15:39.300 --> 01:15:43.890
So now, let's move from the
Windows-side on the edge

01:15:43.890 --> 01:15:45.540
to the cloud with Foundry.

01:15:45.540 --> 01:15:51.300
And we are building Foundry into
this full application platform

01:15:51.300 --> 01:15:52.950
for the agent era, right?

01:15:52.950 --> 01:15:57.660
I mean, every era of the
platform shift when we moved

01:15:57.660 --> 01:15:59.910
to the cloud, we had the
Cloud-native App stack,

01:15:59.910 --> 01:16:03.630
and now we have the Agent-native
App stack in Foundry.

01:16:03.630 --> 01:16:09.180
We are particularly excited
about the Foundry-hosted agent

01:16:09.180 --> 01:16:12.660
as a runtime for
long-running agents.

01:16:12.660 --> 01:16:15.180
Agents now have -- so,
if you're building

01:16:15.180 --> 01:16:20.640
in Foundry-hosted agents, you
can have all the IQ layers.

01:16:20.640 --> 01:16:22.200
You have the tools.

01:16:22.200 --> 01:16:26.790
You have the durability and
the memory and the state.

01:16:26.790 --> 01:16:28.200
You have your own sandbox.

01:16:28.200 --> 01:16:33.300
In fact, it's a super-fast
sandbox that you can spin up.

01:16:33.300 --> 01:16:38.760
You can generate the rubrics,
you can -- and the evals.

01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:41.940
You can, in fact, have all
the safety and the guardrails

01:16:41.940 --> 01:16:44.490
around your agentic system.

01:16:44.490 --> 01:16:46.440
And, in fact, even one
of the coolest things

01:16:46.440 --> 01:16:48.084
in Foundry is,
it's a continuously

01:16:48.117 --> 01:16:49.560
improving loop even, right?

01:16:49.560 --> 01:16:52.080
It's got that self-improvement
loop built in, right?

01:16:52.080 --> 01:16:56.220
So you build an agent that's
continuously getting better.

01:16:56.220 --> 01:17:01.380
And I'm really also excited
today to announce a partnership

01:17:01.380 --> 01:17:04.680
with Fireworks AI,
bringing, in fact,

01:17:04.680 --> 01:17:08.610
all of their open-weight
models to Foundry.

01:17:08.610 --> 01:17:11.850
That means giving you, as
developers, more choices,

01:17:11.850 --> 01:17:14.070
as well as their
great inference stack

01:17:14.070 --> 01:17:17.400
to build the next generation
of these agentic applications

01:17:17.400 --> 01:17:21.720
with all the enterprise
rails that Foundry has,

01:17:21.720 --> 01:17:24.060
as well as the
governance Foundry has.

01:17:24.060 --> 01:17:26.115
So, really excited about
that partnership.

01:17:26.149 --> 01:17:30.509
[ Applause ]

01:17:30.543 --> 01:17:34.000
And so, now that brings
us to the tools.

01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:37.360
GitHub itself is at
the heart of all this.

01:17:37.360 --> 01:17:39.700
You know, in fact,
Jensen spoke to this;

01:17:39.700 --> 01:17:42.240
The GitHub is not just
about the code repo,

01:17:42.240 --> 01:17:46.160
it's becoming the control
plane for all the agents.

01:17:46.160 --> 01:17:49.160
And nearly everything
we measure on GitHub,

01:17:49.160 --> 01:17:53.840
whether it's repo creation, PR
activity, API usage, actions,

01:17:53.840 --> 01:17:56.480
all of them are growing faster

01:17:56.480 --> 01:17:59.100
because of these
agentic workflows.

01:17:59.100 --> 01:18:02.130
This new scale is
driven by humans

01:18:02.130 --> 01:18:04.470
and agents
collaborating together.

01:18:04.470 --> 01:18:08.670
And we are exposing our tooling
across every form factor.

01:18:08.670 --> 01:18:14.375
In fact, we have seen tremendous
growth in CLI-driven --

01:18:14.409 --> 01:18:17.250
you know, the approachability
of the CLI form factor.

01:18:17.250 --> 01:18:20.070
It's always been great
to go to a terminal.

01:18:20.070 --> 01:18:23.040
And now, though, when combined
with the power of the models

01:18:23.040 --> 01:18:25.500
and natural language, you
know, CLI has become the thing

01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:27.060
that everybody goes to.

01:18:27.060 --> 01:18:33.120
But at the end of the day, when
you have hundreds of CLIs,

01:18:33.120 --> 01:18:35.160
it becomes pretty complicated.

01:18:35.160 --> 01:18:39.270
It doesn't scale, especially
the cognitive load that I have

01:18:39.270 --> 01:18:43.860
when you have 100 CLI sessions
open is such that you kind

01:18:43.860 --> 01:18:45.030
of need something new.

01:18:45.030 --> 01:18:49.320
And so, that's what has led
us to build, you know,

01:18:49.320 --> 01:18:53.730
we need like this tool,
essentially, that has the speed

01:18:53.730 --> 01:18:59.040
and the flexibility of a CLI,
but has the capability of an IDE

01:18:59.040 --> 01:19:00.300
and the ability to scale

01:19:00.300 --> 01:19:03.390
to infinite number
of agent sessions.

01:19:03.390 --> 01:19:06.720
And so, today we are taking
that next big step,

01:19:06.720 --> 01:19:10.097
introducing our new
GitHub Copilot app.

01:19:10.131 --> 01:19:15.098
[ Applause ]

01:19:15.132 --> 01:19:17.220
And we realize that it's
not sufficient

01:19:17.220 --> 01:19:19.590
because you still have a
backend to deal with, right?

01:19:19.590 --> 01:19:22.110
Code is easy to generate,
but what about the backend?

01:19:22.110 --> 01:19:24.600
So you need to contend
with identities,

01:19:24.600 --> 01:19:27.330
storage, database schemas.

01:19:27.330 --> 01:19:30.630
And that's why we are really
super excited about Rayfin.

01:19:30.630 --> 01:19:36.810
Rayfin is an agent-first SDK
that connects your agents

01:19:36.810 --> 01:19:39.330
to a backend-as-a-service

01:19:39.330 --> 01:19:42.660
and we are bringing this
to everywhere you build.

01:19:42.660 --> 01:19:46.010
That's why I'm, again,
super excited about Rayfin

01:19:46.010 --> 01:19:48.200
and the partnership with Replit.

01:19:48.200 --> 01:19:52.105
You can now build apps in
Replit while the -- yeah.

01:19:52.139 --> 01:19:55.030
[ Applause ]

01:19:55.030 --> 01:19:57.190
You can build the app
in Replit while the app

01:19:57.190 --> 01:20:01.390
and data are deployed into the
enterprise-managed fabric tenant

01:20:01.390 --> 01:20:04.620
thanks to the Rayfin SDK.

01:20:04.620 --> 01:20:08.490
And so, this Rayfin SDK is
available now for anyone else

01:20:08.490 --> 01:20:11.310
to be able to use with
their tools as a backend

01:20:11.310 --> 01:20:13.800
and essentially have
this backend service.

01:20:13.800 --> 01:20:17.880
And now to show you all
of the Foundry and Rayfin

01:20:17.880 --> 01:20:20.550
and building agents and
the long-running agents,

01:20:20.550 --> 01:20:22.320
let me invite up
on stage Cassidy.

01:20:22.320 --> 01:20:23.497
Cassidy, take it away.

01:20:23.531 --> 01:20:25.181
[ Cheering ]

01:20:25.214 --> 01:20:26.498
[ Applause ]

01:20:26.532 --> 01:20:29.010
Cassidy Williams:
Hello, everyone.

01:20:29.010 --> 01:20:31.350
I'm so excited to be amongst
my fellow devs today.

01:20:31.350 --> 01:20:32.790
This has been a long day.

01:20:32.790 --> 01:20:34.830
Fix your shoulders,
sit back, all right.

01:20:34.830 --> 01:20:36.210
I see a lot of you sitting up.

01:20:36.210 --> 01:20:37.260
Great, great.

01:20:37.260 --> 01:20:39.780
I know you're drinking from the
firehose of information today,

01:20:39.780 --> 01:20:42.330
so I want you to go into the
next few minutes thinking,

01:20:42.330 --> 01:20:45.660
"What can I try out on
my laptop later today?"

01:20:45.660 --> 01:20:48.240
So, as Satya said, we're going
to show you Rayfin, but first,

01:20:48.240 --> 01:20:52.230
I can't wait to show you
the new GitHub Copilot app.

01:20:52.230 --> 01:20:55.110
This app is your home base for
development and operations

01:20:55.110 --> 01:20:57.270
on your computer, and we think
you're going to love it.

01:20:57.270 --> 01:20:58.890
So, let me show you around.

01:20:58.890 --> 01:21:00.240
When you open up the
app from the start,

01:21:00.240 --> 01:21:01.620
you see this home screen here

01:21:01.620 --> 01:21:04.140
where you can kick off a
new agentic coding session.

01:21:04.140 --> 01:21:06.720
But also, before I get
into the serious stuff,

01:21:06.720 --> 01:21:10.080
you can drag Mona around,
and there's a game.

01:21:10.080 --> 01:21:12.770
Look, it's so fun.

01:21:12.770 --> 01:21:15.830
Okay, I'm not very good at it
so let's just get back to --

01:21:15.830 --> 01:21:19.250
you can kick off a new
agentic coding session.

01:21:19.250 --> 01:21:22.560
So, I started off one a
little bit earlier here,

01:21:22.560 --> 01:21:25.590
and it gave me a review of a
bunch of release blockers.

01:21:25.590 --> 01:21:26.700
Which one should I fix?

01:21:26.700 --> 01:21:28.310
Call it out. . .

01:21:29.070 --> 01:21:31.710
Eight? Three?
The "critical" ones?

01:21:31.710 --> 01:21:32.580
You know what?

01:21:32.580 --> 01:21:34.399
How about we just
do all of them?

01:21:35.430 --> 01:21:36.360
Let's go.

01:21:36.360 --> 01:21:41.160
This app will now kick off a separate
session for every single issue here.

01:21:41.160 --> 01:21:44.190
I don't have to worry about
stashing or coding complex

01:21:44.190 --> 01:21:45.720
or anything, because
the app takes care

01:21:45.720 --> 01:21:48.270
of that, with git work trees.

01:21:48.270 --> 01:21:50.970
Git-worktrees are isolated
environments for each session

01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:53.190
that you run so your
agents can work in parallel

01:21:53.190 --> 01:21:55.080
without stepping on each other.

01:21:55.080 --> 01:21:57.420
But you still have to
merge them, right?

01:21:57.420 --> 01:22:00.810
So, Copilot has your
back there too.

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:04.950
If I head over, not to this
one, but to this issue here,

01:22:04.950 --> 01:22:07.110
I can run "Agent merge".

01:22:07.110 --> 01:22:08.850
And when I enable "Agent merge",

01:22:08.850 --> 01:22:11.340
Copilot will continuously
babysit this PR

01:22:11.340 --> 01:22:15.090
