01:26:08.370 --> 01:26:09.840
Thank you so much.
Happy Pride.

01:26:09.840 --> 01:26:10.902
Back to you, Satya.

01:26:10.936 --> 01:26:12.276
[ Cheering ]

01:26:12.309 --> 01:26:17.609
[ Applause ]

01:26:17.643 --> 01:26:19.160
Satya Nadella:
All right.

01:26:19.160 --> 01:26:21.950
Now we're back to
IDEs that have UI.

01:26:21.950 --> 01:26:24.950
That's sort of so cool.

01:26:24.950 --> 01:26:28.380
You know, it kind of
comes a full circle.

01:26:28.380 --> 01:26:32.250
Now let's talk about how
you can observe, govern,

01:26:32.250 --> 01:26:34.530
and secure these agents.

01:26:34.530 --> 01:26:38.760
Agent 365 is the
agent control plane.

01:26:38.760 --> 01:26:44.870
Agent requires their own
identities, access controls,

01:26:44.870 --> 01:26:47.068
even when they're working
on your behalf, right?

01:26:47.102 --> 01:26:48.920
You just want that
work on behalf,

01:26:48.920 --> 01:26:53.570
identity to be enforced,
so we extended Entra.

01:26:53.570 --> 01:26:58.790
Agents need real-time defense,
so we extended Defender.

01:26:58.790 --> 01:27:03.350
Agents require this always-on
data protections and compliance,

01:27:03.350 --> 01:27:05.820
so we extended Purview.

01:27:05.820 --> 01:27:08.580
And these agents can
be hosted anywhere.

01:27:08.580 --> 01:27:12.600
They can be on AWS,
GCP, not just on Azure,

01:27:12.600 --> 01:27:14.760
or built with any framework.

01:27:14.760 --> 01:27:18.930
And today we're announcing
a number of updates,

01:27:18.930 --> 01:27:22.910
including the GA
of Agent 365 SDK.

01:27:22.910 --> 01:27:26.780
And we're expanding it to your
local agents running on Windows

01:27:26.780 --> 01:27:30.170
and elsewhere and the "claws"
you just saw earlier.

01:27:30.170 --> 01:27:35.180
And let us take a look at how
all of Agent 365 composes.

01:27:35.214 --> 01:27:37.174
Over to you, Amanda.

01:27:39.200 --> 01:27:40.906
[ Applause ]

01:27:40.940 --> 01:27:43.110
AMANDA FOSTER: Everyone
is building agents.

01:27:43.110 --> 01:27:45.360
But that's not the
hard part anymore.

01:27:45.360 --> 01:27:48.150
The hard part is integrating
them into your business

01:27:48.150 --> 01:27:50.010
and governing them at-scale.

01:27:50.010 --> 01:27:53.760
Today, I'll show you how
Foundry makes this easy.

01:27:53.760 --> 01:27:55.320
Let's start locally.

01:27:55.320 --> 01:27:57.330
I've already built
a LangGraph agent.

01:27:57.330 --> 01:28:00.510
And now, I'll show you
the value Foundry adds.

01:28:00.510 --> 01:28:02.580
First, tools.

01:28:02.580 --> 01:28:04.830
Agents need tools
to get work done.

01:28:04.830 --> 01:28:07.920
And with Foundry Toolbox,
I just add my tools once

01:28:07.920 --> 01:28:13.110
and any agent can consume them
through a single MCP endpoint.

01:28:13.110 --> 01:28:15.510
And because tools
live centrally,

01:28:15.510 --> 01:28:17.820
that means governance
does as well.

01:28:17.820 --> 01:28:21.180
I've applied a guardrail,
which blocks PII from leaking

01:28:21.180 --> 01:28:23.700
into tool calls and
tool responses.

01:28:23.700 --> 01:28:26.430
And all I have to do
is apply this once,

01:28:26.430 --> 01:28:29.520
and all my agents are protected.

01:28:29.520 --> 01:28:32.970
Now, let's make this
agent enterprise-ready.

01:28:32.970 --> 01:28:34.980
To deploy this agent to Foundry

01:28:34.980 --> 01:28:37.740
all I have to do is add
this one block of code;

01:28:37.740 --> 01:28:39.210
and then I push my changes,

01:28:39.210 --> 01:28:42.240
and GitHub Actions
takes it from there.

01:28:42.240 --> 01:28:45.330
Once deployed, I can
actually use this agent

01:28:45.330 --> 01:28:48.810
in the same Foundry extension
I showed you earlier,

01:28:48.810 --> 01:28:51.840
and let's now test it out.

01:28:51.840 --> 01:28:54.360
I'm asking it to track
a few open items

01:28:54.360 --> 01:28:56.670
from a stand-up I
had earlier today.

01:28:56.670 --> 01:29:00.330
And what's actually happening
right now is Foundry is spinning

01:29:00.330 --> 01:29:04.110
up a dedicated micro VM
just for this session.

01:29:04.110 --> 01:29:08.850
And the session even gets its
own persistent file system.

01:29:08.850 --> 01:29:11.430
What you're going to see in a
minute here, is if I go to the

01:29:11.430 --> 01:29:14.760
"Files" tab to track
the open items,

01:29:14.760 --> 01:29:18.390
the agent's actually
writing to a file.

01:29:18.390 --> 01:29:21.000
Pretty cool, right?

01:29:21.000 --> 01:29:24.720
Plus, Foundry now has
server-side traces

01:29:24.720 --> 01:29:28.110
and built-in evals to show
me exactly what happened

01:29:28.110 --> 01:29:29.910
on every run.

01:29:29.910 --> 01:29:33.900
But how do I know if my
agent's doing a good job?

01:29:33.900 --> 01:29:38.100
That's what Foundry's brand new
rubric evaluators are for.

01:29:38.100 --> 01:29:39.540
It's super simple.

01:29:39.540 --> 01:29:43.950
With just one AZD command,
Foundry reads my agent

01:29:43.950 --> 01:29:47.580
and then generates the
evaluation criteria for me.

01:29:47.580 --> 01:29:51.060
In other words, it creates
a rubric personalized just

01:29:51.060 --> 01:29:52.500
to this agent.

01:29:52.500 --> 01:29:56.430
Now let's check out this
rubric in Foundry Portal.

01:29:56.430 --> 01:29:59.490
Look at these dimensions.

01:29:59.490 --> 01:30:03.030
Governance outcome correctness,
prescribed source usage.

01:30:03.030 --> 01:30:04.920
I didn't write any of these.

01:30:04.920 --> 01:30:08.040
Foundry generated them
from production traces.

01:30:08.040 --> 01:30:12.420
And with this rubric, I can
score my agent and run evals.

01:30:12.420 --> 01:30:15.300
But we can do so
much more than that.

01:30:15.300 --> 01:30:19.620
That's where Foundry's brand-new
agent optimizer takes over.

01:30:19.620 --> 01:30:20.820
Here's how it works.

01:30:20.820 --> 01:30:22.320
It tunes four things:

01:30:22.320 --> 01:30:26.610
the model, instructions, tool
descriptions, and skills.

01:30:26.610 --> 01:30:28.920
And then it generates,
improves candidates,

01:30:28.920 --> 01:30:33.030
and scores each one using the
rubric I just showed you.

01:30:33.030 --> 01:30:34.710
Here, I can view the candidates.

01:30:34.710 --> 01:30:36.330
I can see the strategy used.

01:30:36.330 --> 01:30:37.800
I can see their scores.

01:30:37.800 --> 01:30:41.400
And I can actually view
exactly what changed.

01:30:41.400 --> 01:30:43.680
This candidate, for
example, improved its score

01:30:43.680 --> 01:30:46.860
by updating the model
and the system prompt.

01:30:46.860 --> 01:30:50.670
Foundry then makes it super easy
to deploy the best candidate

01:30:50.670 --> 01:30:53.640
as a brand-new agent version.

01:30:53.640 --> 01:30:56.490
But this is not a
one-time thing.

01:30:56.490 --> 01:30:58.860
Every run feeds the next eval,

01:30:58.860 --> 01:31:03.210
and every eval tells the
optimizer where to improve next.

01:31:03.210 --> 01:31:06.720
So, your agents now get better
the more they're used.

01:31:06.720 --> 01:31:09.960
But now, let's put
this agent to work.

01:31:09.960 --> 01:31:14.550
I've published my agent to
Teams and M365 Copilot.

01:31:14.550 --> 01:31:17.610
And right now, I'm going
to ask it to just catch me

01:31:17.610 --> 01:31:18.600
up on what I've missed

01:31:18.600 --> 01:31:23.550
because I've been offline all
morning but my agent hasn't.

01:31:23.550 --> 01:31:25.230
While it works on that, though,

01:31:25.230 --> 01:31:28.350
let me explain what makes
this agent different.

01:31:28.350 --> 01:31:30.360
This is an autopilot agent

01:31:30.360 --> 01:31:32.460
which means it has
its own identity

01:31:32.460 --> 01:31:34.140
and productivity license.

01:31:34.140 --> 01:31:38.490
So, it can work across
M365 on its own behalf.

01:31:38.490 --> 01:31:41.760
Earlier, you saw me using
this agent by myself,

01:31:41.760 --> 01:31:45.720
but shipping a feature in a new
release takes a whole team.

01:31:45.720 --> 01:31:49.020
And that's why this agent
lives in our Teams group Chat.

01:31:49.020 --> 01:31:50.640
Now let's check out how it did.

01:31:50.640 --> 01:31:52.890
It summarized all
updates and called

01:31:52.890 --> 01:31:56.340
out the key features I
need to be tracking.

01:31:56.340 --> 01:31:59.700
But to put an agent like
this, actually to work,

01:31:59.700 --> 01:32:03.330
in your enterprise, governance
needs to come first.

01:32:03.330 --> 01:32:07.680
And that's why every autopilot
agent requires admin approval.

01:32:07.680 --> 01:32:10.440
Here, admins can review
all critical details,

01:32:10.440 --> 01:32:12.720
and they can even choose
who has the ability

01:32:12.720 --> 01:32:14.370
to talk to the agent.

01:32:14.370 --> 01:32:16.050
But it doesn't end there.

01:32:16.050 --> 01:32:20.460
Even after approval, admins can
continue to monitor the agent

01:32:20.460 --> 01:32:22.800
and block it at any time.

01:32:22.800 --> 01:32:27.900
But governance cannot
apply to just one agent.

01:32:27.900 --> 01:32:31.170
Every agent in your organization
needs to be managed

01:32:31.170 --> 01:32:34.800
with the same rigor as
users, apps, and devices.

01:32:34.800 --> 01:32:39.060
And that's exactly what
Microsoft Agent 365 provides.

01:32:39.060 --> 01:32:41.250
So, let's zoom out.

01:32:41.250 --> 01:32:45.150
Today we saw Foundry accelerate
development, take your agent

01:32:45.150 --> 01:32:50.220
from local to enterprise-ready,
and put it to work in M365.

01:32:50.220 --> 01:32:51.660
Foundry makes it simple.

01:32:51.660 --> 01:32:53.940
You build the agent,
we handle the rest.

01:32:53.940 --> 01:32:55.912
Now back to you, Satya.

01:32:55.946 --> 01:32:57.890
[ Applause ]

01:32:57.924 --> 01:32:58.742
Satya Nadella:
Thank you, Amanda.

01:32:58.776 --> 01:33:01.766
[ Applause ]

01:33:01.800 --> 01:33:06.570
You know, what you just saw was
how we're building security

01:33:06.570 --> 01:33:08.430
for AI.

01:33:08.430 --> 01:33:11.670
But there is also one other
critical aspect, especially,

01:33:11.670 --> 01:33:15.090
you know, the news today
is all about, you know,

01:33:15.090 --> 01:33:20.940
how do you defend yourself using
AI against attacks that may,

01:33:20.940 --> 01:33:22.590
in fact, be using AI, right?

01:33:22.590 --> 01:33:28.740
So, last month we announced our
multi-model agentic security

01:33:28.740 --> 01:33:30.000
system, MDASH.

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:34.410
That's essentially an agent
harness for security,

01:33:34.410 --> 01:33:36.270
essentially, that we built.

01:33:36.270 --> 01:33:41.310
We're bringing together 100
agents across theFrontier

01:33:41.310 --> 01:33:45.630
and custom models to really find
these exploitable bugs better

01:33:45.630 --> 01:33:47.700
than any single model does.

01:33:47.700 --> 01:33:51.030
In fact, when we debuted this
harness, it was on the top

01:33:51.030 --> 01:33:53.250
of CyberGym benchmark.

01:33:53.250 --> 01:33:57.900
So, I want you to take a look
at what you can do with MDASH

01:33:57.900 --> 01:34:00.450
to really defend
against AI attacks

01:34:00.450 --> 01:34:02.160
and defend the entire
digital estate.

01:34:02.160 --> 01:34:04.520
Over to you, Sarah.

01:34:04.520 --> 01:34:09.697
[ Applause ]

01:34:09.731 --> 01:34:10.950
Sarah Young:
Thanks, Satya.

01:34:10.950 --> 01:34:13.980
Now, we all know that security
scans can take a while,

01:34:13.980 --> 01:34:16.260
so I'm not going to do one live.

01:34:16.260 --> 01:34:17.490
So, let me show you the results

01:34:17.490 --> 01:34:21.810
of an MDASH scan I already
ran on my code base.

01:34:21.810 --> 01:34:24.750
Now, the system runs
as a standalone CLI,

01:34:24.750 --> 01:34:27.960
but today I'm using it
in my GitHub Copilot app

01:34:27.960 --> 01:34:30.180
on my local dev machine.

01:34:30.180 --> 01:34:33.510
The scan is broken down
by vulnerability domains

01:34:33.510 --> 01:34:37.710
and severity, and it's also,

01:34:37.710 --> 01:34:40.680
additional to finding
traditional issues

01:34:40.680 --> 01:34:43.170
like coding errors and
hard-coded secrets,

01:34:43.170 --> 01:34:46.800
it's also identifying
AI-specific vulnerabilities

01:34:46.800 --> 01:34:48.210
in the code base.

01:34:48.210 --> 01:34:50.220
Now, what happened
under the hood is

01:34:50.220 --> 01:34:53.760
that over 100 specialized
agents are working together

01:34:53.760 --> 01:34:57.390
to discover, debate,
and prove exploitable

01:34:57.390 --> 01:34:59.700
vulnerabilities end-to-end.

01:34:59.700 --> 01:35:03.660
And when the scan finishes,
it generates both a SARIF log

01:35:03.660 --> 01:35:07.530
and an HTML report that I
can give to my management.

01:35:07.530 --> 01:35:11.040
Now, the "defender details"
command allows me to dig

01:35:11.040 --> 01:35:12.810
into these vulnerabilities,

01:35:12.810 --> 01:35:16.200
and I can see what
the vulnerability is,

01:35:16.200 --> 01:35:20.820
where it is in my code, and the
severity to help me prioritize.

01:35:20.820 --> 01:35:25.230
And from here, of course,
we're going to fix it.

01:35:25.230 --> 01:35:27.960
Now, using the
"defender fix" command,

01:35:27.960 --> 01:35:31.320
the system will remediate
suggested fixes directly

01:35:31.320 --> 01:35:33.600
in my local dev environment.

01:35:33.600 --> 01:35:36.264
And when that's done, I
can check out the "diff".

01:35:36.300 --> 01:35:40.200
So, I have full transparency
about what the harness has done,

01:35:40.200 --> 01:35:43.890
and I still have a
human-in-the-loop check.

01:35:43.890 --> 01:35:46.350
But, of course,
everything I've shown you

01:35:46.350 --> 01:35:51.750
so far has run locally, but I
can also create a PR to plug

01:35:51.750 --> 01:35:55.350
into my existing workflows
and push up to my repo.

01:35:55.350 --> 01:35:58.470
And I can take the SARIF
output from the scan,

01:35:58.470 --> 01:36:02.550
and I can upload it to tools
like GitHub Advanced Security

01:36:02.550 --> 01:36:05.790
and manage everything
alongside my other application

01:36:05.790 --> 01:36:07.710
security findings.

01:36:07.710 --> 01:36:10.920
Now, I've shown you MDASH
working on my code,

01:36:10.920 --> 01:36:14.100
but let me show you a
vulnerability our security

01:36:14.100 --> 01:36:17.310
research teams
identified using MDASH

01:36:17.310 --> 01:36:19.590
that you can go and
look up yourself.

01:36:19.590 --> 01:36:23.850
So, the TLDR, because this is
a lot to read, of this bug is

01:36:23.850 --> 01:36:29.340
that WASM time reads an
out-of-date map of an object,

01:36:29.340 --> 01:36:32.460
runs off the end, and
then it crashes the host.

01:36:32.460 --> 01:36:35.400
So, this is exactly
the kind of bug

01:36:35.400 --> 01:36:39.090
that the harness was built for,
because the flow is spread

01:36:39.090 --> 01:36:41.820
across three different
parts of the code base.

01:36:41.820 --> 01:36:45.180
So, no single file looks
wrong on its own.

01:36:45.180 --> 01:36:49.980
They look absolutely fine,
and we can even see here,

01:36:49.980 --> 01:36:52.440
which is probably my
favorite part of this bug,

01:36:52.440 --> 01:36:54.150
a very confident statement

01:36:54.150 --> 01:36:57.480
from the developers claiming
everything is fine.

01:36:57.480 --> 01:37:00.120
Now, this is exactly
the sort of reassurance

01:37:00.120 --> 01:37:03.900
that fools normal scanners
and single AI models.

01:37:03.900 --> 01:37:05.640
But MDASH wasn't fooled.

01:37:05.640 --> 01:37:08.580
One team of agents spotted
the suspicious gap,

01:37:08.580 --> 01:37:10.860
another team argued it apart,

01:37:10.860 --> 01:37:13.740
and a third team built
a working example

01:37:13.740 --> 01:37:16.020
that actually
triggered the crash.

01:37:16.020 --> 01:37:18.720
And it did all of this in
an open-source code base.

01:37:18.720 --> 01:37:20.880
And this is the kind
of joined-up reasoning

01:37:20.880 --> 01:37:24.420
that previously required
significant manual security

01:37:24.420 --> 01:37:26.070
research effort.

01:37:26.070 --> 01:37:29.340
This is MDASH helping
developers create secure code

01:37:29.340 --> 01:37:32.700
from the start, coming
soon to your CLI

01:37:32.700 --> 01:37:34.800
and the Microsoft
Defender portal.

01:37:34.800 --> 01:37:36.010
Back to you, Satya.

