02:10:18.540 --> 02:10:24.900
To close out, though, I want to
talk about that Frontiers beyond

02:10:24.900 --> 02:10:28.770
that push this ecosystem
of ours and really say,

02:10:28.770 --> 02:10:30.900
"What's the next big thing?"

02:10:30.900 --> 02:10:34.680
Bending the curve -- you know,
when Mustafa talked about this

02:10:34.680 --> 02:10:37.381
with Gianrico when
it comes to health --

02:10:37.414 --> 02:10:38.790
but building, you know,

02:10:38.790 --> 02:10:44.220
that scientific discovery loop
can have, perhaps, the biggest

02:10:44.220 --> 02:10:46.410
societal impact, right?

02:10:46.410 --> 02:10:50.190
Science today still is
a little too linear.

02:10:50.190 --> 02:10:53.640
You create a hypothesis --
you form a hypothesis,

02:10:53.640 --> 02:10:56.520
you run an experiment,
you wait for lab results,

02:10:56.520 --> 02:10:59.760
which is out of band, and
then you begin again.

02:10:59.760 --> 02:11:03.240
But what if the scientific
method itself could become more

02:11:03.240 --> 02:11:06.900
continuous, more parallel,
more programmable?

02:11:06.900 --> 02:11:09.630
That's what we're doing with
Microsoft Discovery, right?

02:11:09.630 --> 02:11:13.500
Discovery brings together
the models, the HPC compute,

02:11:13.500 --> 02:11:16.830
the knowledge graphs of all
the scientific knowledge,

02:11:16.830 --> 02:11:22.860
the automated lab and simulation
into one agentic discovery loop.

02:11:22.860 --> 02:11:25.290
And so, today I'm
really thrilled

02:11:25.290 --> 02:11:29.310
to GA Microsoft Discovery,
and to show you all of this

02:11:29.310 --> 02:11:31.770
in action; let me invite
up David on stage.

02:11:31.770 --> 02:11:32.998
David, take it away.

02:11:33.032 --> 02:11:34.310
[ Applause ]

02:11:34.344 --> 02:11:35.070
David Carmona:
Hello!

02:11:35.104 --> 02:11:36.507
[ Applause ]

02:11:36.541 --> 02:11:39.960
I work in the Microsoft
Discovery and Quantum team.

02:11:39.960 --> 02:11:43.200
Today, to recycle a PET
plastic like this bottle,

02:11:43.200 --> 02:11:45.810
you have to shred
it and melt it.

02:11:45.810 --> 02:11:47.400
The result is degraded.

02:11:47.400 --> 02:11:49.800
You cannot use it to
make this bottle again.

02:11:49.800 --> 02:11:51.720
It's downcycling.

02:11:51.720 --> 02:11:54.240
Cambridge Consultants,
part of Capgemini,

02:11:54.240 --> 02:11:57.240
is using Microsoft Discovery
to advance that research.

02:11:57.240 --> 02:11:58.901
Let me show it to you. . .

02:11:59.880 --> 02:12:02.250
This is the Microsoft
Discovery app.

02:12:02.250 --> 02:12:06.450
It is based on VS Code because
agentic discovery has many

02:12:06.450 --> 02:12:09.240
parallels with agentic
software engineering.

02:12:09.240 --> 02:12:13.620
Today, I want to do these
three things as a scientist.

02:12:13.620 --> 02:12:16.290
First, I want to write
a scientific paper

02:12:16.290 --> 02:12:17.550
about this topic.

02:12:17.550 --> 02:12:20.040
I'll explore an existing
line of research.

02:12:20.040 --> 02:12:24.300
Instead of melting the
plastic, I can use proteins

02:12:24.300 --> 02:12:29.040
to the composite so you can
recycle it again and again.

02:12:29.040 --> 02:12:33.450
Second, I want to perform
the actual discovery

02:12:33.450 --> 02:12:35.250
to find new proteins.

02:12:35.250 --> 02:12:39.300
And third, I want to
create a lab protocol

02:12:39.300 --> 02:12:42.420
to test the results
in a real lab.

02:12:42.420 --> 02:12:43.560
If you are a developer,

02:12:43.560 --> 02:12:46.980
these three steps should
be very familiar to you.

02:12:46.980 --> 02:12:50.610
They look a lot like
planning, coding and testing,

02:12:50.610 --> 02:12:53.700
and deploying to production,
but for science.

02:12:53.700 --> 02:12:56.160
Let's launch it.

02:12:56.160 --> 02:12:58.380
This will kick off
the discovery engine.

02:12:58.380 --> 02:13:00.390
You can think of this as a team

02:13:00.390 --> 02:13:03.240
of specialized agents
always running

02:13:03.240 --> 02:13:06.360
in the background following
the scientific method.

02:13:06.360 --> 02:13:10.050
You can see those agents
here and you can add more.

02:13:10.050 --> 02:13:14.160
Microsoft Discovery includes a
community of agents, models,

02:13:14.160 --> 02:13:17.070
and tools across many domains.

02:13:17.070 --> 02:13:21.930
You can use open-source,
third-parties, or create your own.

02:13:21.930 --> 02:13:24.780
Okay, so, this is still running
and it will be running

02:13:24.780 --> 02:13:29.190
for a while, even hours or days,
because the discovery engine,

02:13:29.190 --> 02:13:32.190
like science, is not sequential.

02:13:32.190 --> 02:13:33.930
It is exploring hypotheses

02:13:33.930 --> 02:13:38.040
and performing long-running
simulations dynamically.

02:13:38.040 --> 02:13:40.800
So, let me open one that
is already completed.

02:13:40.800 --> 02:13:43.590
You can see the files
that it created here.

02:13:43.590 --> 02:13:48.180
This one is the research
paper that I was asking for.

02:13:48.180 --> 02:13:52.380
To create it, Discovery is using
a knowledge graph internally,

02:13:52.380 --> 02:13:55.320
bringing together public
scientific literature

02:13:55.320 --> 02:13:57.150
and internal knowledge.

02:13:57.150 --> 02:14:01.710
This is a critical asset because
it provides complete visibility

02:14:01.710 --> 02:14:05.640
of the reasoning so scientists
can be in full control.

02:14:05.640 --> 02:14:08.910
We can go now to step two,
the discovery itself.

02:14:08.910 --> 02:14:11.700
Let me open the
output for that one.

02:14:11.700 --> 02:14:17.160
So, how do you come up with new
proteins to decompose plastic?

02:14:17.160 --> 02:14:19.650
This is the approach
that Discovery took.

02:14:19.650 --> 02:14:21.810
First, you need an AI model

02:14:21.810 --> 02:14:25.140
to predict how good a
protein is for that.

02:14:25.140 --> 02:14:28.410
Then, you need a way to
generate new proteins.

02:14:28.410 --> 02:14:29.880
And finally, you need

02:14:29.880 --> 02:14:32.820
to identify the most
promising ones.

02:14:32.820 --> 02:14:34.650
So, let's start with the model.

02:14:34.650 --> 02:14:37.680
Microsoft Discovery
trained multiple models

02:14:37.680 --> 02:14:38.940
and picked the best.

02:14:38.940 --> 02:14:41.790
And there's no out-of-the-box
agent for that.

02:14:41.790 --> 02:14:43.050
But that's okay.

02:14:43.050 --> 02:14:46.290
If Microsoft Discovery does
not find an agent for a task,

02:14:46.290 --> 02:14:50.280
it will create one on the
fly, which is pretty cool.

02:14:50.280 --> 02:14:53.610
So, here's all the
files that it created.

02:14:53.610 --> 02:14:56.370
This one here is the
YAML for the agent.

02:14:56.370 --> 02:14:57.810
And this one, for example,

02:14:57.810 --> 02:15:00.630
is the Python code
to train the models.

02:15:00.630 --> 02:15:03.840
Now, we need to generate
candidates for new proteins.

02:15:03.840 --> 02:15:07.320
And that task requires
a lot of compute.

02:15:07.320 --> 02:15:11.790
Discovery is integrated with
HPC, so agents can use it

02:15:11.790 --> 02:15:13.800
for complex simulations.

02:15:13.800 --> 02:15:15.990
You can see the process here.

02:15:15.990 --> 02:15:20.160
It started with a seed protein
and then it created variations

02:15:20.160 --> 02:15:22.380
by replacing small segments.

02:15:22.380 --> 02:15:24.630
You can see those
segments there.

02:15:24.630 --> 02:15:26.760
Then it applied the
model from before

02:15:26.760 --> 02:15:31.230
to see the variation helps or
not, learning in that process.

02:15:31.230 --> 02:15:34.650
This is done millions of
times in multiple jobs

02:15:34.650 --> 02:15:39.000
in parallel exploring a
huge tree of proteins.

02:15:39.000 --> 02:15:43.860
The result is 80 proteins that
are ready to be sent to the lab

02:15:43.860 --> 02:15:45.960
for testing, which
we said it was

02:15:45.960 --> 02:15:48.180
like deploying software, right?

02:15:48.180 --> 02:15:49.170
The problem, though, is

02:15:49.170 --> 02:15:53.250
that creating a protein is a
little bit more complicated.

02:15:53.250 --> 02:15:57.030
The most common way is
inserting DNA into bacteria

02:15:57.030 --> 02:16:00.000
so the bacteria create
the protein for me.

02:16:00.000 --> 02:16:04.500
So, Discovery created another
file, this one here.

02:16:04.500 --> 02:16:07.110
This file contains
all the DNA sequences

02:16:07.110 --> 02:16:08.850
to create each protein.

02:16:08.850 --> 02:16:10.770
I can send this now to a lab,

02:16:10.770 --> 02:16:13.170
create the proteins
and test them.

02:16:13.170 --> 02:16:16.770
Or, I can go one step further.

02:16:16.770 --> 02:16:18.480
If I have an automated lab,

02:16:18.480 --> 02:16:21.690
I can integrate it
directly with the agents.

02:16:21.690 --> 02:16:22.710
Let's do it.

02:16:22.710 --> 02:16:24.960
So, let me go
back to the session

02:16:24.960 --> 02:16:28.260
and let's just
submit job to lab.

02:16:28.260 --> 02:16:29.340
Go.

02:16:29.340 --> 02:16:32.520
This will use a custom agent
that is sending instructions

02:16:32.520 --> 02:16:33.990
for the lab equipment.

02:16:33.990 --> 02:16:35.850
And this is very real.

02:16:35.850 --> 02:16:39.300
Cambridge Consultants does
have an automated lab.

02:16:39.300 --> 02:16:41.880
And I have it right here.

02:16:41.880 --> 02:16:44.400
So, this is the application
control in the lab.

02:16:44.400 --> 02:16:48.090
It has a Copilot interface so
the scientists can interact

02:16:48.090 --> 02:16:50.460
with the lab to design
experiments, which is,

02:16:50.460 --> 02:16:53.400
it feels like being Iron
Man but for chemistry.

02:16:53.400 --> 02:16:56.040
In this case, Discovery
is admitting the job.

02:16:56.040 --> 02:16:57.660
You can see it already here.

02:16:57.660 --> 02:17:00.120
But remember, you need
to grow the bacteria

02:17:00.120 --> 02:17:01.320
so this will take some time.

02:17:01.320 --> 02:17:03.750
So, let me open a previous run.

02:17:03.750 --> 02:17:04.770
And there you go.

02:17:04.770 --> 02:17:08.280
These are all the steps
that the agents are doing.

02:17:08.280 --> 02:17:10.770
Most of them
completely automated

02:17:10.770 --> 02:17:12.420
with human supervision.

02:17:12.420 --> 02:17:14.380
How cool is that?

02:17:14.414 --> 02:17:20.260
[ Applause ]

02:17:20.260 --> 02:17:22.540
This is bringing together
the physical world

02:17:22.540 --> 02:17:25.860
and the digital agents in
a unified discovery loop.

02:17:25.860 --> 02:17:29.850
And this was just one example of
what Microsoft Discovery can do.

02:17:29.850 --> 02:17:32.820
Customers across industries
are using it today

02:17:32.820 --> 02:17:35.580
to embrace a new era of
scientific discovery.

02:17:35.580 --> 02:17:36.540
Thank you.

02:17:36.540 --> 02:17:37.699
Back to you, Satya.

02:17:37.733 --> 02:17:41.513
[ Applause ]

02:17:41.547 --> 02:17:43.420
Satya Nadella: Thank
you so much, David.

02:17:43.420 --> 02:17:47.940
Talking about scientific
discovery, we're also continuing

02:17:47.940 --> 02:17:51.960
to make rapid progress
on our long-term goal

02:17:51.960 --> 02:17:55.213
of building a scalable
quantum computer.

02:17:55.247 --> 02:17:59.880
We announced last
year our first QPU.

02:17:59.880 --> 02:18:02.370
We created a new state of matter

02:18:02.370 --> 02:18:05.460
that was only theorized
100 years ago

02:18:05.460 --> 02:18:08.100
and we proved it
out that it exists.

02:18:08.100 --> 02:18:12.840
Our vision was to take a very
radically different approach

02:18:12.840 --> 02:18:16.230
to addressing the
fundamental barriers

02:18:16.230 --> 02:18:18.840
to building a scalable
quantum machine, which is all

02:18:18.840 --> 02:18:23.460
about reliability,
speed, as well as size.

02:18:23.460 --> 02:18:26.670
Since then, we have
continued to make progress

02:18:26.670 --> 02:18:30.960
across the full quantum
stack with both our academic

02:18:30.960 --> 02:18:32.100
and industry partners.

02:18:32.100 --> 02:18:35.850
In fact, in QuNorth, we will
have a quantum computer powered

02:18:35.850 --> 02:18:39.180
by atom computers,
natural atom computers,

02:18:39.180 --> 02:18:40.800
with our stack in there.

02:18:40.800 --> 02:18:42.600
We are also working
with Algorithmic

02:18:42.600 --> 02:18:45.420
and Columbia and ETH Zurich.

02:18:45.420 --> 02:18:51.030
And we ourselves, in fact, use
this discovery agentic loop

02:18:51.030 --> 02:18:56.160
to accelerate the work in
quantum, compressing years

02:18:56.160 --> 02:18:59.040
of research into this last year.

02:18:59.040 --> 02:19:04.710
And today, I'm really thrilled
to announce Majorana 2.

02:19:04.710 --> 02:19:08.040
And so, this is Majorana 2.

02:19:08.040 --> 02:19:13.770
Majorana 2 implements the
next-generation material stack

02:19:13.770 --> 02:19:16.580
that we use discovery
to discover

02:19:16.580 --> 02:19:18.500
and build and help fabricate.

02:19:18.500 --> 02:19:21.980
The resulting qubits are
exceptionally reliable

02:19:21.980 --> 02:19:26.510
and capable of maintaining
their state much longer.

02:19:26.510 --> 02:19:29.570
While other common approaches
deliver a lifetime

02:19:29.570 --> 02:19:32.240
of just microseconds
or even milliseconds,

02:19:32.240 --> 02:19:38.390
Majorana 2 provides qubit mean
lifetime of 20 seconds or up

02:19:38.390 --> 02:19:42.360
to even a minute, essentially
a thousand times higher

02:19:42.360 --> 02:19:45.520
than what we were able to
achieve with Majorana 1.

02:19:45.520 --> 02:19:47.396
[ Applause ]

02:19:47.430 --> 02:19:50.180
And the operations,
and this is so key,

02:19:50.180 --> 02:19:53.640
operations in Majorana
2 are one microsecond,

02:19:53.640 --> 02:19:59.550
enabling pretty complex, quantum
computation in that lifetime.

02:19:59.550 --> 02:20:04.650
And all of this in the same
qubit form factor of Majorana 1

02:20:04.650 --> 02:20:05.880
in one one-hundredth

02:20:05.880 --> 02:20:08.220
of a millimeter
controlled all digitally,

02:20:08.220 --> 02:20:12.420
which is again a super important
aspect, making it all possible

02:20:12.420 --> 02:20:14.850
to fit a million of these qubits

02:20:14.850 --> 02:20:17.760
in a chip smaller
than a credit card.

02:20:17.760 --> 02:20:21.990
It's this combination of
the reliability, the speed,

02:20:21.990 --> 02:20:26.610
the size that makes the
topological approach so unique.

02:20:26.610 --> 02:20:30.900
With Majorana 1, we had proven
out the foundational physics,

02:20:30.900 --> 02:20:36.180
and with Majorana 2, now we
begin the engineering scale.

02:20:36.180 --> 02:20:37.804
But ultimately -- yeah.

02:20:37.838 --> 02:20:41.710
[ Applause ]

02:20:41.710 --> 02:20:47.700
But ultimately, it is never
about tech for tech's sake.

02:20:47.700 --> 02:20:51.450
It's about tackling those
pressing challenges

02:20:51.450 --> 02:20:54.180
of people and planet.

02:20:54.180 --> 02:20:57.810
It is also the fundamental
point of this conference.

02:20:57.810 --> 02:21:01.470
The question is not whether you
can build the next-grade model,

02:21:01.470 --> 02:21:05.580
the next-grade platform, or
even this quantum machine.

02:21:05.580 --> 02:21:08.730
The question is, how do
we build this Frontier

02:21:08.730 --> 02:21:11.130
ecosystem together?

02:21:11.130 --> 02:21:15.000
Because there are really
two stories people can tell

02:21:15.000 --> 02:21:16.800
about this moment.

02:21:16.800 --> 02:21:19.980
One is that technology
concentrates power,

02:21:19.980 --> 02:21:23.730
reduces human agency,
and leaves the society

02:21:23.730 --> 02:21:26.520
to absorb the consequences.

02:21:26.520 --> 02:21:31.470
The other is that we use this
next wave to unlock opportunity

02:21:31.470 --> 02:21:33.180
for developers, scientists,

02:21:33.180 --> 02:21:35.820
enterprises, and
every community.

02:21:35.820 --> 02:21:39.270
And our job is to make
the second story true.

02:21:39.270 --> 02:21:42.630
That's our North Star for
the Frontier ecosystem.

02:21:42.630 --> 02:21:44.010
Let's all go build together.

02:21:44.010 --> 02:21:45.523
Thank you all very, very much.

02:21:45.557 --> 02:21:52.700
[ Applause ]

02:21:52.734 --> 02:22:34.570
[ Music ]

02:22:34.570 --> 02:22:35.860
David Shaw: This is one
of those rare moments

02:22:35.860 --> 02:22:40.000
when something that once felt
beyond imagination suddenly

02:22:40.000 --> 02:22:43.120
feels within reach.

02:22:43.120 --> 02:22:45.140
[ Music ]

02:22:45.140 --> 02:22:47.700
Quantum is our most
fundamental physical theory.

02:22:47.700 --> 02:22:49.980
It's really the low-level
operating system

02:22:49.980 --> 02:22:51.930
of the real physical world.

02:22:51.930 --> 02:22:53.160
Sabrina Maniscalco:
For quantum computing

02:22:53.160 --> 02:22:55.950
to deliver real-world impact,

02:22:55.950 --> 02:23:00.040
it has to be both
reliable and scalable.

02:23:00.040 --> 02:23:01.002
[ Music ]

02:23:01.036 --> 02:23:03.030
David Shaw: Reaching quantum
at scale will allow us

02:23:03.030 --> 02:23:04.800
to take a more direct approach

02:23:04.800 --> 02:23:06.720
to solving problems
in chemistry --

02:23:06.720 --> 02:23:08.490
Sabrina Maniscalco: The
discovery of new material.

02:23:08.490 --> 02:23:09.480
David Shaw: Life sciences.

02:23:09.480 --> 02:23:09.930
Sabrina Maniscalco: The way

02:23:09.930 --> 02:23:13.380
in which we bring
drugs to market.

02:23:13.380 --> 02:23:16.350
It opens up millions
of possibilities.

02:23:16.350 --> 02:23:18.330
Quantum will change the world

02:23:18.364 --> 02:23:20.998
and this change is just
beginning to happen.

